@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@cindex philosophy
Roadmaps rot because they live somewhere the work does not. A @file{ROADMAP.md}
maintained by hand drifts from reality within a month. An issue tracker on
another website is invisible from the terminal, unavailable offline, and cannot
be reviewed alongside the change that closes it.

cairn takes the position that project state is source. Every item is a plain
Markdown file with YAML frontmatter, stored in the repository, versioned with
the code. There is no server, no account, and no network access of any kind;
the only program cairn ever starts is your editor, and any hooks you configure.

@cindex schema
What distinguishes cairn from a directory of notes is the schema. A single
file, @file{cairn.toml}, declares the item types, the statuses they move
through, any additional fields, the milestones, and saved views. The commands
enforce that schema, so the structure survives contact with many contributors
--- and with programs, which is the harder case.

@section What an item is

@cindex intent
@cindex decision record
Describing cairn as an issue manager is accurate and undersells it enough to
produce the wrong decisions.

An item is a @dfn{record of intent}. It carries the reasoning that produced it
--- the problem, the proposal, the costs that were weighed, and how you will
know when it is done --- it lives with the code it describes, and it is worth
@emph{more} after it closes than before, because it is then the answer to why
something is the way it is.

That is true of an architecture decision record and false of a ticket. A ticket
points at a conversation that happened somewhere else, and is worthless the
moment it is closed. Scheduling --- statuses, milestones, what is ready to start
--- exists here to make the record actionable, not the other way round.

@subsection What follows from it

This is not a slogan. It decides things.

Items carry reasoning rather than titles, which is why a type has a
@code{template} that seeds headings, and why that is a feature rather than
friction. It is why @command{cairn log} was worth building: a record whose
history cannot be read is a worse record. It is why the file format is specified
for a horizon longer than this program's. And it is why the answer to ``can
filing be made faster by dropping the body'' is no.

@subsection What cairn should therefore refuse

@cindex comments
A record of intent does not need comment threads, reactions, or a notification
when somebody begins typing. Those belong to the conversation, and the
conversation is not the record --- it is the thing the record exists to survive.

That is a concrete refusal, and it is the first place to point when somebody
asks for a discussion feature. @xref{The promise}, for the other boundary.

@noindent
cairn is @emph{not}:

@itemize @bullet
@item
a replacement for a code forge. It has no pull requests and no notifications.

@item
a general task manager. It is scoped to one repository.

@item
a synchronisation service. Items are files; they travel however your files
travel, which is usually @command{git}.
@end itemize

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@example
cargo install cairn
@end example

@noindent
Or from a source tree:

@example
cargo install --path .
@end example

@cindex completions
@cindex man page
Shell completions and manual pages are generated by the program itself:

@example
cairn completions bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/cairn
cairn completions zsh  > ~/.zfunc/_cairn
cairn man --dir /usr/local/share/man/man1
@end example

@noindent
@command{cairn completions} also accepts @code{fish}, @code{elvish} and
@code{powershell}.

@node Platforms
@chapter Platforms

@cindex platforms
@cindex support tiers
A tier is a statement about what continuous integration does, not about
intent.

@table @strong
@item Tier 1 --- Linux
@code{x86_64} and @code{aarch64}, built against musl so one binary works on any
distribution. The full test suite runs on every change, and again against the
musl target; release binaries are published. This is the reference platform:
where two platforms disagree and the standards do not settle it, Linux
behaviour is the definition.

@item Tier 2 --- macOS and Windows
@code{aarch64} and @code{x86_64} on macOS, @code{x86_64} on Windows. The full
test suite runs on every change and release binaries are published. A failure
here is a bug, not a caveat.

@item Tier 3 --- everything else
Any other target Rust supports. cairn is expected to build, but nothing is
tested and no binaries are published.
@end table

@cindex portability, of the test suite
The end-to-end suite drives the compiled binary from Rust rather than from a
shell, so it runs unmodified on every tier 1 and tier 2 platform. An earlier
suite written in POSIX shell did not run on Windows at all, which meant a
third of the supported platforms was covered by unit tests only.

@section Where platforms genuinely differ

@cindex line endings
Two behaviours are unavoidably platform-shaped.

Hook strings are interpreted by the platform shell, and shells disagree. The
array form of a hook avoids the shell entirely and is portable; @xref{Hooks}.

@code{project.filename_max} defaults to 255 bytes, the POSIX-typical maximum
filename length. Filesystems with tighter limits need it lowered; @xref{Configuration}.

@node Durability
@chapter Durability

@cindex durability
@cindex atomic writes
The repository is the database, which puts the whole weight of the design on
the write path.

@section Atomic writes

Every file cairn writes is written to a temporary file beside the target,
flushed to the device, and renamed over it. Rename is atomic within a
filesystem on every supported platform, so an interruption at any point leaves
either the previous contents or the new ones. Nothing is ever truncated in
place. On systems that support it the containing directory is flushed too, so
the rename itself survives a power failure and not merely the bytes.

@section Line endings

@cindex line endings
@cindex CRLF
@cindex core.autocrlf
Item files are read with either line ending and written back with the one they
arrived with. Without this, a checkout configured with @code{core.autocrlf}
would have every write reformat the whole file, and a backlog shared between
platforms would churn continuously.

New items are written with line feeds, and @command{cairn init} installs a
@file{.gitattributes} pinning the item directory to @code{eol=lf}, so a
repository has a single answer regardless of how each contributor's client is
configured.

@section A damaged file

@cindex partial reads
Read-only commands --- @command{list}, @command{next}, @command{board},
@command{search}, @command{roadmap} --- report a file they cannot parse and
continue with the rest. A backlog should not become unreadable because one file
is mid-edit.

Everything else refuses. @command{check}, @command{render}, @command{export} and
every command that writes stop with an error, because acting on a partial view
of the backlog is how data is lost: rendering a roadmap that silently omits an
item, or allocating an id that a broken file already holds.

@section Concurrent writers

@cindex locking
@cindex concurrency
@cindex agents, concurrent
Allocating an identifier means reading every one in use and adding one, which is
correct only while nothing else is doing the same thing. That was a safe
assumption while the writer was a person at a terminal. It stopped being one
when two agents sharing a backlog became an ordinary arrangement.

Commands that write take a lock — a file created in the item directory with an
operation that fails if it already exists, which is an atomic test-and-set
everywhere cairn runs. Commands that only read never take it: a backlog must
stay listable while somebody else is writing to it.

A writer that cannot get the lock within ten seconds stops and says which file
it was waiting on. A lock older than five minutes is assumed to belong to a
process that died and is broken with a warning; the window is wide enough that a
slow import is never mistaken for a corpse.

@cindex hooks, and the lock
The lock is released before hooks run. A hook may call @command{cairn} itself,
and by the time it fires the write is already durable, so there is nothing left
to protect and holding the lock would only let a hook block on its own parent.

The lock file lives beside the items, where the item scan already ignores
dotfiles, and @command{cairn init} writes a @file{.gitignore} for it so it never
reaches the repository.

@section Merging branches

@cindex merging
@cindex git integration
@cindex merge driver
Two branches that each add an item conflict in the rendered roadmap and collide
on an identifier, on the first merge. Neither is really a conflict. The roadmap
is generated, so reconciling two renderings of different inputs is meaningless;
the identifiers collide because allocation reads the highest in use, which no
branch can know.

@example
cairn init --git
@end example

@noindent
registers a merge driver for the generated file and installs a @code{post-merge}
hook that renumbers any collision and renders again. It is safe on a project
that already exists --- that is how one adopts it --- and doing it twice changes
nothing.

@cindex index order, and merging
The order git works in constrains the design. Paths are resolved in index order,
and @file{ROADMAP.md} sorts before @file{cairn/items/}, so when the merge driver
runs the items it would render from are still unmerged. The driver therefore
does not pretend to know the answer: it keeps our side, which is a valid
rendering of a real state, and the hook replaces it with the right one once the
items are settled.

@cindex hooks, not cloned
Two consequences worth knowing. The hook lives in @file{.git/hooks}, which git
does not clone, so each working copy runs @command{cairn init --git} once. And a
merge performed by a forge rather than locally runs no hook at all, which is why
@command{cairn check --render} belongs in continuous integration regardless.

Anything the hook changed is left for a person to commit; a hook that amended
the merge commit would be doing something nobody asked it to.

@section Which side keeps a contested identifier

@cindex merge collision
@cindex renumber
Two branches that each create an item allocate the same identifier, because
allocation is one more than the highest in use and no branch can know what the
other did. When they meet, one of the two has to move.

They are not equals. One side has been published --- it is on the branch people
pull, and anybody may have written its identifier into a commit message or a
link --- and the other is arriving. Renaming the published one churns history
and breaks those references, so the published one keeps its number.

cairn asks the repository which is which. If the current commit is a merge, its
@emph{first parent} is the branch the merge was made on, and an item whose file
was added on that side was already published. If a merge is in progress and has
not been committed, @code{HEAD} is that side.

Asking whether @code{HEAD} is a merge @emph{before} asking whether one is in
progress matters, because the @code{post-merge} hook runs after the merge commit
exists but while @file{.git/MERGE_HEAD} is still on disk. Asking the other way
round picks the merge commit, which contains both sides, and concludes that both
were published.

Neither the creation date nor the depth of history distinguishes them: two
branch commits made a moment apart tie on the first, and siblings tie on the
second. Both were tried.

Outside a repository, or on an ordinary commit, the older item keeps the
identifier and the filename breaks a tie --- which is what cairn did before, and
is the best that can be done with no history to consult.

@section What a merge resolves, and what it does not

@cindex merge driver
@cindex conflicts
Two branches that both touched the backlog meet in three kinds of file, and
cairn treats each differently.

@table @asis
@item The generated roadmap
Re-derived. Two renderings of different inputs cannot be reconciled, and there
is nothing in it that is not in the items.

@item Identifiers
Repaired by @command{renumber}, keeping the number on the side already
published. @xref{Identifiers}.

@item Item files
Resolved only where the disagreement has an answer.
@end table

@subsection The two rules, and no others

A @strong{sequence} merges by union. Two branches each adding a dependency, a
label, or a second thing an item is part of both meant what they added, and
neither meant to remove the other's.

The union is aware of the common ancestor, so a value one side deliberately
@emph{removed} stays removed rather than being restored by the other side
simply not having touched it. It is also order-stable, because an unchanged item
that becomes a diff would churn the tree and fail a @option{--check} in
continuous integration for no reason.

@code{updated} takes the later date, because it is a stamp rather than a
statement.

@subsection Everything else stays a conflict

Two people saying different things about one fact --- a priority, a status, a
title, a body --- is not a merge to guess at. cairn writes the usual markers and
a person decides.

That conservatism is the point. The reason the driver can be trusted with
generated files is that it only ever touches what it could rebuild from
scratch; an item cannot be rebuilt, so the bar for resolving anything in one is
higher rather than lower. A driver that resolved too much would lose an edit
somebody meant, silently, which is worse than any conflict.

@section Interrupted renumbering

@command{cairn renumber} moves a file aside before writing it back under its new
id. If it is interrupted in between, the staged file is all that remains of the
item. The next command to read the backlog restores it and says so. If something
already occupies the original name, cairn refuses to overwrite it and reports
both paths.

@section What is tested

@cindex property tests
The frontmatter parser handles input typed by people, generated by models, and
carried in from other trackers, so it is property-tested rather than only
exercised by examples: whatever cairn writes it reads back unchanged, rendering
the same item twice is byte-identical, line endings survive a round trip, and
arbitrary bytes may be rejected but never cause a panic.

Atomicity is tested by killing cairn during a write, repeatedly, and checking
the item is still whole. Concurrency is tested by racing forty processes to
create items, which must yield forty distinct identifiers, and twelve to claim
one item, which must yield exactly one holder and eleven clear refusals.

@node File format
@chapter File format

@cindex file format, specification
@cindex specification, standalone
The normative specification lives in @file{spec/README.md}, and at
@url{https://github.com/oddurs/cairn/blob/main/spec/README.md}. It stands alone:
a reader for the format can be implemented from it without consulting cairn at
all, and cairn is its reference implementation rather than its definition.

This chapter describes the same format for someone already reading the manual.
Where the two disagree, the specification is right.

@section Structure

An item is a file whose name ends in @file{.md}. It consists of, in order:

@enumerate
@item
A line containing exactly @samp{---}, optionally preceded by a UTF-8 byte order
mark.
@item
Zero or more lines of YAML, the @dfn{frontmatter}.
@item
A line containing exactly @samp{---} or @samp{...} after trailing whitespace is
removed, the @dfn{closing delimiter}. The @emph{first} such line ends the
frontmatter; later ones are body text.
@item
The @dfn{body}, which is arbitrary text and is never interpreted.
@end enumerate

A file that does not begin with an opening delimiter, or that has no closing
delimiter, is not an item and must be rejected.

Lines are separated by either LF or CRLF. A reader accepts both. A writer
reproduces whichever the file used, and uses LF for a file it creates.

@section Keys

The frontmatter is a YAML mapping. Every key is optional except as noted.

@table @code
@item id
Unsigned integer, unique within a project. Required, except that a reader may
take it from a leading run of digits in the filename when the key is absent.
@item title
String. Required in practice: an item without one is reported by
@command{cairn check}.
@item type
String naming a declared type.
@item status
String naming a declared status. Required.
@item milestone
String naming a declared milestone.
@item assignee
String.
@item labels
Sequence of strings. A reader must also accept a single string, splitting it on
commas and discarding surrounding whitespace.
@item depends_on
Sequence of unsigned integers. A reader must also accept a single string of
comma-separated integers, and must accept each element with an optional leading
@samp{#}.
@item created
@itemx updated
Date as @samp{YYYY-MM-DD}.
@item source
String recording where an imported item came from, conventionally
@samp{@var{system}:@var{locator}}. Used to make repeated imports idempotent.
@end table

Any other key is a @dfn{custom field}. Its value may be any YAML scalar or
sequence. A project declares the custom fields it expects; an undeclared key is
reported as a warning and never as an error.

@cindex unknown keys
@strong{A reader must preserve keys it does not recognise.} This is what allows
a project written by a newer cairn to be opened, edited and written back by an
older one without silently losing data. It is not a nicety; it is the reason
version skew is survivable at all.

@section Ordering and layout

A writer emits keys in the order listed above, followed by custom fields in the
order they were read, so that rewriting an unchanged item produces an identical
file and a changed one produces a minimal diff.

Files are named @file{@var{id}-@var{slug}.md}, where @var{slug} is the title
reduced to lowercase alphanumerics separated by single hyphens, shortened only
as far as @code{project.filename_max} requires. Nothing depends on the name: the
@code{id} key is authoritative when present. Subdirectories are searched.
Entries beginning with @samp{.} or @samp{_}, and @file{README.md}, are not
items.

@section YAML scalars

@cindex YAML, scalar resolution
YAML resolves unquoted scalars before any of this applies. A hand-written
@samp{0x1F} is a number and will be read as @samp{31}; @samp{12:30} and
@samp{1.20} are likewise subject to YAML's own rules. cairn quotes anything it
writes that would otherwise change meaning, so this affects only files written
by hand or by another tool. Quote such values.

@node Compatibility
@chapter Compatibility

@cindex compatibility
@cindex format version
Data outlives the program that wrote it. A project records the format it uses,
and this is the promise attached to that number.

@example
format = 1
@end example

@section The rules

@enumerate
@item
A patch or minor release may add optional keys. It may do nothing else to the
format.
@item
Removing a key, changing what a key means, or making an optional key required
requires a new format number, a major release, and a migration.
@item
A reader preserves keys it does not recognise.
@item
A project recording a format newer than the reader understands is refused, with
an explanation naming both numbers. It is never read on a best-effort basis:
misreading data is worse than declining to read it.
@end enumerate

@section Migrating

@command{cairn migrate} brings a project to the current format;
@command{cairn migrate --check} reports without changing anything and exits
non-zero if a migration is due, which is what belongs in continuous
integration.

Format 2 moved milestones out of the configuration and into items. No item file
changed, and the migration touches @file{cairn.toml} and writes the new
milestone items, and is described under @ref{Compatibility}.

The command was exercised on every run for as long as there was nothing to
migrate, so that a path first attempted when it is needed was not a path nobody
had tried. That turned out to be worth doing.

@section What would still cost a format number

@cindex format number
@cindex migration
Written down so it is decided rather than discovered.

@multitable @columnfractions .55 .45
@headitem Change @tab Cost
@item @code{id} stops being an unsigned integer
@tab @strong{every item file, every project}
@item Removing @code{milestone} or @code{depends_on} as documented keys
@tab configuration only, as format 2 was
@item @code{created} or @code{updated} gaining a time
@tab every item that has one
@item @code{labels} becoming references rather than strings
@tab every item that has one
@item Any new @emph{optional} key on an @emph{item}
@tab free, no new number
@item Any new key in @file{cairn.toml}
@tab @strong{a new number} --- see below
@item @code{assignee} accepting a sequence as well as a string
@tab free --- @code{labels} already sets that precedent
@end multitable

@noindent
The configuration line is the one that surprises people, so it is worth its own
paragraph.

@cindex configuration
@cindex unknown keys
An item reader preserves keys it does not recognise --- that is §4.1 of the
specification, and it is why an item may gain an optional key for free.
@file{cairn.toml} is the opposite: an unrecognised key is refused, and the whole
project with it. That is deliberate. A key cairn does not know in an item came
from somewhere and dropping it loses data; a key cairn does not know in the
configuration is a typo, and ignoring it would let a project believe it had
configured something it had not.

The consequence is that a new configuration key is a format change, whatever it
costs. Adding one at the same number would make every cairn already in the field
refuse the project outright --- not degrade, refuse --- which is exactly what a
format number exists to prevent.

@noindent
Of the item-shaped changes, only the first is expensive, and it is the only one
whose cost grows with adoption. Everything else is either free or shaped like format 2, which touched
no item file at all.

That asymmetry is why the identifier question is a decision to settle rather
than a feature to schedule: the cheapest moment to change what an identifier is
was yesterday, and the second cheapest is today.

@section Roles: a decision taken by not taking it

@cindex roles
@cindex field names
@cindex vocabulary
Every documented key is also a word. A project whose people are
@emph{maintainers} still writes @code{assignee:}; a project with no releases
still writes @code{milestone:} or nothing at all. That is a promise about
vocabulary, and vocabulary is the part of a schema most likely to be wrong for
somebody. Changing one of those words costs a format number.

There is a mechanism that would make it cost nothing. A field would declare the
@emph{role} it plays, and the role, not the name, would be what the format
promises:

@example
[[field]]
name = "maintainer"
role = "assignee"      # this project's word for the assignee role
@end example

@noindent
A small closed set of roles --- identity, title, state, scheduled-for,
blocked-by, composed-into, worker, owner, provenance --- and each project maps
its own names onto them. It is the trick behind status categories
(@pxref{Configuration}) moved one level up: a fixed axis underneath, chosen names
above.

cairn does not do this, and the reasoning is recorded here rather than left as
an absence.

@cindex grep
The case against is that it costs the thing that makes these files worth
having. Today anyone can @command{grep} for @code{assignee:} across every cairn
project in the world and get the right answer. Under roles they would first have
to read a configuration file to learn what this project calls it, and a format
whose meaning requires a lookup is a worse format. Nobody has asked for it,
either: the names have not yet been wrong for anybody. Building a mechanism for
a problem that has not occurred is how a schema becomes a framework.

@cindex compatibility promise
Most of all, @strong{waiting costs nothing}. A @code{role} attribute would be a
new optional key, and by the rules above a new optional key is free --- no
format number, no migration, no item file touched. This is one of the rare
decisions that does not get more expensive by being deferred, which is the whole
argument for deferring it.

If it is ever taken, the shape to take is the cheap one: the names stay
@emph{defaults} rather than promises. A field called @code{assignee} plays the
assignee role without saying so, and only a project that wants a different word
declares one. Grep keeps working for everybody who never touches it.

@cindex milestone
This is not only a hypothetical about some future project. cairn looks up
@code{milestone} by that literal name --- in the reference machinery, in
@command{list}, @command{show}, @command{import} and @command{init}, and as the
default @code{render.group_by}. Rename the field and the roadmap has nothing to
group by; @command{cairn roadmap} and @command{cairn check} both say so rather
than quietly drawing nothing, but the name is load-bearing and pretending
otherwise would be the dishonest version of this decision.

@quotation
@strong{The condition that would reverse this.} A real project whose word for
one of these is wrong enough that its people keep having to translate --- not a
preference, a recurring translation. That is the evidence this decision is
waiting for, and until it appears the absence of the mechanism is the feature.
@end quotation

@section What a format bump may cost you

@cindex compatibility promise
@quotation
A format bump may cost you a command. It must never cost you your data, and it
must never cost you the ability to look.
@end quotation

@noindent
A project written by an older cairn is read, not refused. Every command that
only reads --- @command{list}, @command{show}, @command{next}, @command{search},
@command{board}, @command{roadmap}, @command{log}, @command{export},
@command{config}, @command{check}, @command{agent} --- works against it, and
works @emph{correctly}: cairn knows what an older format meant, so an older
project's roadmap is the roadmap its own cairn would have drawn.

Writing is where it stops, at the lock every mutating command takes and no read
ever does. The refusal names @command{cairn migrate}, and a single line on
standard error says the project is behind --- on standard error, so a script
reading @option{--json} is unaffected.

A project written by a @emph{newer} cairn is still refused outright, for reading
as well as writing. That is what §8 of the specification is about, and it is
right: reading a format nobody has seen on a best-effort basis means misreading
data in ways nobody can predict.

@section What is guaranteed by test

@cindex golden corpus
A corpus of item files lives in @file{tests/golden}, each with the values it
must parse to. It includes files cairn would not write itself: bare strings
where a sequence belongs, a missing id, CRLF endings, keys from a version that
does not exist. Every run checks them. Changing one of those expectations is a
deliberate act, and by the rules above it is a format change.

@cindex frozen corpus
That corpus tests today against today, which is not the thing that breaks
people. So beside it sits one directory per format that has ever existed ---
@file{tests/golden/format-1} and so on --- holding items as that format wrote
them, with the values they parsed to @emph{then}.

Three things are checked on every run, and one at build time:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Every format still parses. A file written by any cairn that ever existed is
read correctly by this one --- values, not merely the absence of an error.
@item
Migrating an older corpus produces the current corpus's expectations exactly,
which is the only thing that makes a format bump honest.
@item
An older corpus is frozen. A digest of each one is asserted, so a case cannot be
edited to make a later reader agree with it. If a case is wrong, the fix is a
new case in the current corpus; the old one is the record of how that format
actually read.
@item
A format cannot arrive without its corpus: @code{CURRENT_FORMAT} is read from
the source, and a missing @file{format-N} directory fails the build on the bump
rather than on the release.
@end itemize

@noindent
The second reader (@pxref{File format}) runs against every format too, not
only the newest. A specification that describes only the current format is not a
specification of the format; it is a specification of the moment.

@section Where the defects have actually come from

@cindex testing
@cindex soak test
@cindex fuzzing
This is recorded because it decided how the suite is built. Of the defects
found in cairn so far, close to none were found by the example tests --- the
ones written in advance, describing a situation somebody had already thought
of. They came from a soak test, from a platform the author cannot run, from an
adversarial reader, and from attempting a release.

Example tests are not therefore worthless: they are the regression net, and
every defect found by another method leaves one behind. But they only ever check
what somebody already imagined, and the bugs have been elsewhere. So the suite
spends where the returns have been.

@table @asis
@item The soak test
@file{tests/soak.rs} drives cairn through a long arbitrary sequence of ordinary
operations --- every mutating command, the MCP server, an export and import
round trip --- and checks after every single step that the backlog still holds
together. It prints its seed, and a failure is reproduced by passing that seed
back.

A second test races several processes writing at once, because identifiers are
allocated as one more than the highest in use and that is only safe while a lock
makes the read and the write one step.

@example
make soak
CAIRN_SOAK_SEED=7 CAIRN_SOAK_OPS=2000 make soak
@end example

@item Properties
Some things are true of every input rather than of a chosen one. What cairn
writes it reads back unchanged; a body carrying CRLF is normalised on write; a
backlog that leaves through @command{export} comes back through
@command{import} with its dependencies still pointing at the same items; and
the filter grammar obeys laws --- negation partitions the backlog, alternation
is union, a comma is intersection and does not care about order.

The dependency property earned its place immediately: it found that
@command{import} was silently dropping every dependency, which nothing failed
on and @command{check} was perfectly happy with.

@item Fuzzing the command line
Every argument surface takes text from a person or from a model, and a model
gets them wrong in ways a person would not think to try.
@file{tests/fuzz_args.rs} drives the real binary with arbitrary argument
vectors and asserts one thing: cairn may refuse anything, but it may not panic.

@example
CAIRN_FUZZ_ROUNDS=20000 cargo test --release --test fuzz_args
@end example

@item Rules
@file{tests/rules.rs} guards decisions that are invisible in the code --- an
absence, or an agreement between files. @xref{The promise}.
@end table

@node The promise
@chapter The promise

@cindex promise
@cindex boundary
@cindex free software
The previous chapter is a promise about the data. This one is the promise about
the project. It is reproduced word for word in @file{PROMISE.md} and in the
README, and a test fails if the three ever disagree.

@quotation
@c promise:begin
cairn is free software under the MIT licence, and will remain so.

Everything a single repository can do is part of cairn, and is free: items, the
schema, the board, the roadmap, agents, merging, import and export. No
capability that belongs in cairn will be held back for something else. If
anything ever built beside cairn disappeared tomorrow, no cairn user would be
affected.

cairn does everything a single repository can do, and nothing beyond it. That
is not a policy but a description. A repository cannot see other repositories.
It cannot serve somebody who has not cloned it. It has no notion of who is
permitted to close an item, and it cannot tell you something changed while you
were not looking. Those are not capabilities withheld from cairn; they are
capabilities a directory of files does not have.

So cairn will never grow accounts, authentication, or remotes. The day that
cairn login exists, this promise has been broken.
@c promise:end
@end quotation

@section Why it is written down this early

Every project that changed its licence did so after it had something to lose.
The line moved once the old position became expensive, and the people who had
built on the old position mostly forked rather than follow. What those projects
had in common was declaring the boundary late.

cairn has no users and nothing to sell, so declaring it now costs nothing, which
is precisely what makes it worth something.

@section The boundary is a design constraint, not a price list

@cindex accounts
@cindex authentication
@cindex remotes
The three things cairn will never grow --- accounts, authentication, remotes ---
are not withheld features. They are the features a directory of files cannot
have, and naming them turns a recurring argument into a settled question.

@table @asis
@item Can cairn notify me when an item changes?
No. A program that runs when you invoke it cannot notice anything while you are
not running it. What you want is whatever watches your repository.

@item Can cairn show me work across all my repositories?
No. Run it in each, or write something that reads the format. Several backlogs
can be combined with @command{cairn export} without cairn needing a concept of
more than one project.

@item Can cairn tell me who is allowed to close this?
No. A repository has no notion of permission. The thing that does is whatever
governs writes to it, which is your forge, not your tracker.
@end table

@noindent
The practical guard is that @command{cairn login} must never exist. A command
line that acquires a session is a client for a service, and everything above
has already been given away by the time anybody notices.

@section What should be built instead

@cindex integrating with cairn
Those needs are real. They are met by something that reads the format rather
than something that links this code --- which is why cairn ships no library
target (@pxref{Integrating}), and why the format is specified independently of
the program that writes it.

@node Integrating
@chapter Integrating

@cindex integrating
@cindex library
@cindex no library target
cairn ships a program and a specification. It deliberately ships no library.

cairn is MIT licensed, so linking it would cost a caller nothing legally. The
reason there is no library is not the licence.

@cindex specification
It is that the specification is a better contract than a Rust API would be. The
format is versioned, has a conformance corpus, is checked by a second
independent reader, and every version of it that has ever existed is still
tested against the current build. A @code{[lib]} would be a parallel promise
with none of that: semver on internal types, breakage on every refactor, and a
standing reason to keep a bad shape because something depends on it. Worse, if
the easy path were @code{use cairn::@dots{}}, nobody would read the format, and
the thing that lets this outlive the implementation would quietly rot.

So the absence of a @code{[lib]} target in @file{Cargo.toml} is not an
oversight, and a test fails if one appears. There are two supported ways in.

@section Read the files

@cindex file format, reading
The file format (@pxref{File format}) is normative and standalone: an
implementation can be written from it without consulting cairn's source, and
the corpus in @file{tests/golden} --- which includes files cairn would never
write, because those are what other tools produce --- doubles as a conformance
suite.

This is the right choice when you want the data: a dashboard, a static site, a
report, a second implementation in another language.

@section Run the program

@cindex JSON output
@cindex exit codes
When you want cairn's behaviour rather than its bytes, run the binary.
@command{export} writes the whole backlog as an interchange document;
@command{list}, @command{next}, @command{search}, @command{show} and
@command{config} take @option{--json}; and every command's exit status
distinguishes success from a project that does not validate.

@example
cairn export                                  # the whole backlog, as JSON
cairn list --json --filter 'category=open,priority=p0'
cairn next --json                             # only what is startable
cairn check --quiet || echo "the backlog does not validate"
@end example

@noindent
Note that this is not a uniform surface: most commands have no @option{--json},
because most of them exist to change something rather than to report it. If you
find yourself wanting machine-readable output from a command that lacks it,
that is worth reporting --- it is a gap rather than a decision.

@noindent
For a program that wants to work the backlog rather than report on it, the
Model Context Protocol server (@pxref{Working the backlog}) offers the same
operations over a JSON-RPC transport.

@section What is not offered

There is no plugin interface, no daemon to connect to, and no way to have cairn
call your code. Those all invert the relationship: they make cairn the host and
you the guest, which is the shape that eventually needs accounts and a session.
@xref{The promise}.

@node Getting started
@chapter Getting started

@cindex initialisation
Run @command{cairn init} at the root of a repository. It writes
@file{cairn.toml} and creates the item directory.

@example
$ cairn init
created cairn.toml
created cairn/items/
@end example

@noindent
@option{--preset minimal} starts from three statuses and nothing else;
the default, @option{--preset standard}, writes a fully commented schema to
delete from. Then:

@example
$ cairn new "Support OAuth login" --type feature --milestone v0.1
created 0001  Support OAuth login
$ cairn set 1 status=doing
$ cairn board
$ cairn render
wrote ROADMAP.md  1 items
@end example

@noindent
@file{ROADMAP.md} is generated. Never edit it: change the items and render
again. @xref{Continuous integration}, for how to make that a rule rather than
a convention.

@node Items
@chapter Items

@cindex item format
@cindex frontmatter
An item is a Markdown file whose first line is @samp{---}, followed by YAML
frontmatter, a closing @samp{---}, and a free-form body.

@example
---
id: 1
title: Support OAuth login
type: feature
status: doing
milestone: v0.1
labels:
  - auth
depends_on:
  - 3
created: 2026-09-04
updated: 2026-09-11
priority: p0
---

## Problem

Password auth is the only option, and enterprise users keep asking for SSO.
@end example

Everything above the closing delimiter is validated against the schema.
Everything below it is yours; cairn never rewrites the body.

@table @code
@item id
An integer, unique within the project. @xref{Identifiers}.

@item title
Required. The filename tracks it: renaming the title renames the file.

@item type
One of the @code{[[type]]} blocks in @file{cairn.toml}, or absent.

@item status
Required. One of the @code{[[status]]} blocks.

@item milestone
The @code{name} of a @code{[[milestone]]} block, or absent.

@item labels
A list. A bare string is accepted and split on commas, so
@samp{labels: auth, backend} and @samp{labels: [auth, backend]} are equivalent.

@item depends_on
A list of item ids.

@item created
@itemx updated
ISO dates, maintained by the program.
@end table

Any other key is a custom field and must be declared with a @code{[[field]]}
block; @command{cairn check} reports undeclared keys as warnings.

@cindex filenames
Files are named @file{@var{id}-@var{slug}.md} inside the item directory.
Subdirectories are searched, so items may be organised into folders if you
want; names beginning with @samp{.} or @samp{_}, and @file{README.md}, are
ignored.

@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration

@cindex cairn.toml
@file{cairn.toml} is the entire configuration surface. cairn looks for it in
the current directory and each parent, so commands work from anywhere inside
the tree. @command{cairn config} prints the resolved schema, and
@command{cairn config --json} prints it in a form other programs can read.

@section project

@table @code
@item name
Project name, used in headings.

@item description
One line, rendered under the title.

@item dir
Where item files live, relative to @file{cairn.toml}. Default
@file{cairn/items}.

@item id_width
Zero-padding width for displayed ids. Default 4, giving @samp{0001}.

@item default_type
@itemx default_status
Applied to new items when the command line does not say otherwise.

@item url
Base URL used by @code{render.link_items} to make rendered ids clickable.

@item filename_max
@cindex filename length
The longest filename the target filesystem accepts, in bytes. Default 255,
the POSIX-typical maximum. Titles are shortened only as far as this requires;
lower it for filesystems with tighter limits, such as eCryptfs at 143.
@end table

@section type

@example
[[type]]
name = "bug"
label = "defect"        # shown instead of the name
icon = "!"
color = "red"
description = "Something behaves incorrectly."
template = """
## What happens

## Reproduction
"""
@end example

@code{template} seeds the body of every new item of that type, so bug reports
arrive with the same headings without anyone having to remember them.

@section status

@example
[[status]]
name = "doing"
label = "in progress"
category = "active"
color = "yellow"
board = true
@end example

@cindex status order
@cindex category
Two properties carry meaning beyond display. @strong{Order} is the column
order on the board and the sort order in listings. @strong{Category} --- one
of @code{open}, @code{active}, @code{done}, @code{dropped} --- is what the
program reasons about: progress bars, checkbox rendering, the default hiding
of finished work, and the target of @command{cairn close}. Because the logic
keys off the category and not the name, you may call your statuses whatever
your project calls them.

Setting @code{board = false} keeps a status out of @command{cairn board}
without removing it.

@section field

@example
[[field]]
name = "priority"
kind = "enum"
values = ["p0", "p1", "p2", "p3"]
default = "p2"
required = false
column = true
description = "p0 is a release blocker."
@end example

@code{kind} is one of @code{text}, @code{enum}, @code{list}, @code{date},
@code{number} or @code{bool}. Enum values are ordered, so
@samp{cairn list --sort priority} sorts by declared severity rather than
alphabetically. @code{column = true} adds the field to the default listing
table and to the tags in the rendered roadmap.

@section milestone

@cindex milestone, as an item
There is no @code{[[milestone]]} block. A milestone is an item, declared by
saying that its type groups work:

@example
[[type]]
name = "milestone"
groups = "one"
inverse = "scheduled"
@end example

@noindent
and created like any other item, which gives it a key from its title:

@example
cairn new "Usable in anger" -t milestone --set key=v0.1 --set due=2026-10-15
cairn list -t milestone
@end example

Milestones are still the sections of the rendered roadmap, ordered by
@code{due}, those without a date last. What changed is that they are items ---
so one has a body, a history, dependencies on other milestones, and everything
else an item has. The mechanism is described under ``Types that group work'',
and what the implied field does under @ref{Fields that name items}.

@code{[[milestone]]} blocks were format 1. They are no longer read, and a
project that still has one is told so by name rather than having it ignored;
@command{cairn migrate} converts them.

@section view

@example
[[view]]
name = "triage"
description = "Items that still need a decision"
filter = "milestone=,category!=done"
sort = "-priority,created"
columns = ["id", "type", "title", "created"]
@end example

A saved query, used as @samp{cairn list --view triage} or
@samp{cairn board --view triage}.

@section render

@example
[render]
target = "ROADMAP.md"
title = "Roadmap"
group_by = "milestone"
include = "category!=dropped"
checkbox = true
show_ids = true
progress = true
group_by_status = true
link_items = false
header = "doc/roadmap-intro.md"
footer = "doc/roadmap-outro.md"
@end example

@code{group_by} accepts any field, not only @code{milestone}: grouping by
@code{assignee} or by a custom @code{area} field produces a different document
from the same items. @code{header} and @code{footer} name Markdown files
spliced above and below the generated body.

@section Milestones

@cindex milestones
A milestone is an item.

@example
---
id: 42
key: v0.1
title: Usable in anger
type: milestone
due: 2026-10-15
status: doing
---

October because the conference is in November, and this is the thing
worth showing there.
@end example

@noindent
And @code{milestone: v0.1} in an item's frontmatter names it by key, exactly as
it always did.

@subsection Why it changed

@cindex format 2
Until format 2 a milestone was a block in @file{cairn.toml}, and everything it
could not do followed from that. It had no body, so the reason for a date had
nowhere to live --- in a tool whose argument is that items carry the thinking
that produced them. It had no history, so @command{cairn log v0.1}, the most
interesting question anybody asks about a milestone, had no answer. It could not
be assigned, searched, depended upon, or closed with criteria.

It also needed a rule of its own, which walked the list backwards so that an
undated milestone could inherit the date of the next dated one. That code
existed only because milestones had no natural order. Items have one.

@subsection What it is not

@cindex part_of
It is deliberately @emph{not} composition. @code{milestone} is single-valued and
@code{part_of} is many-valued, because an item belongs to several larger efforts
and ships in exactly one release. Collapsing them would not remove a redundant
concept; it would discard the constraint that makes a milestone mean anything.

@subsection Order

A roadmap is a sequence, and milestones are items, so they can depend on one
another. The order is that graph first --- anything a milestone waits on comes
before it --- with @code{due} breaking what the graph leaves free and the
identifier breaking what remains. @command{cairn init} wires the scaffolded ones
in sequence, so nobody has to think about it.

This works because a container never appears in @command{cairn next}
(@pxref{Fields that name items}). Without that, milestones depending on each
other would read as blocked work and crowd out the real thing.

@subsection Where they show up

Milestones are containers, so they are absent from @command{cairn next}, the
board, the roadmap's item lists, and an ordinary @command{cairn list}. They
appear when you ask: @command{cairn list -t milestone}, or @option{--all},
which means all.

@subsection Migrating

@cindex cairn migrate
@example
cairn migrate --dry-run
cairn migrate
@end example

@noindent
No item file changes. @code{milestone: v0.1} means what it meant, which is the
whole reason a reference is addressed by key rather than by identifier: the
migration touches @file{cairn.toml} and writes the new milestone items, and
nothing else moves.

Each @code{[[milestone]]} block becomes an item --- its @code{title} the title,
its @code{description} the body, its @code{due} a field --- and they are wired
into a dependency chain in the order they were declared, which is what replaces
the inheritance rule.

@command{cairn milestone} is gone with it. Adding one is
@command{cairn new -t milestone}, listing them is
@command{cairn list -t milestone}, and by the bar in the command review neither
made a real task harder to do without the command.

@section The one key that should not have a default

@cindex category
@cindex status
Every key in @file{cairn.toml} that defaults is presentational --- a missing
@code{color} is a colour nobody chose --- with one exception.

@code{category} is a claim about what a status @emph{means}, and its default is
@code{open}. So this:

@example
[[status]]
name = "shipped"
@end example

@noindent
produces a status that cairn treats as outstanding work. @command{cairn close}
will not target it, progress bars will not count it, and the roadmap will show
finished work as unfinished. Nothing about it is visibly wrong.

@command{cairn check} reports a status that does not declare one, and
@command{cairn config} marks the category it assumed as assumed, so the resolved
schema never presents a guess as a decision.

Requiring it would be better, and it is not free: making an optional key
required needs a format number and a migration (@pxref{Compatibility}). Format 3
is not worth spending on one key, so the requirement waits for a number that is
being spent anyway. The rule survives its first inconvenient instance, which is
the only test a rule like that ever gets.

@section What the configuration does not promise

@cindex configuration
Four things about @file{cairn.toml} are true, load-bearing, and easy to
discover the expensive way. None of them is a defect.

@subsection Order is behaviour

The order of @code{[[status]]} blocks is the column order on
@command{cairn board} and the sort order in every listing. The order of an
enum's @code{values} is what @option{--sort priority} sorts by.

So a tool that rewrote the file alphabetically, or a person tidying it, would
change what cairn does without changing anything they could see. §7 of the
specification says the order is significant and that nothing may reorder it; the
shipped configurations say so in a comment where somebody is standing when it
matters.

@subsection The configuration is deliberately outside the merge driver

@cindex merge driver
@command{cairn git} registers the driver for the item files and for the rendered
roadmap, and not for @file{cairn.toml}.

That is the decision, not an oversight. Unioning two branches' schema blocks
would produce a schema neither branch wrote --- a status list in an order nobody
chose, a field declared twice with different types --- and a schema conflict
wants a person. It does mean this is the one file where a bad merge is quiet
rather than loud, which is worth knowing before it happens.

@subsection Some keys are semantic and some are decoration

@code{category}, @code{values}, @code{required}, @code{acyclic}, @code{rollup},
@code{target} and @code{by} change what a reader must conclude about an item.
@code{color}, @code{icon}, @code{label}, @code{board} and @code{column} change
what a reader draws.

The file presents both in the same block with the same weight, so the
specification says which half is binding: the category, and nothing else
(§7). A second implementation may ignore every appearance key and conform.

@subsection Multiplicity is spelled twice

@code{kind = "list"} for an ordinary field, @code{cardinality = "many"} for a
reference. The same idea under two names, and no way to say a many-valued enum.

@code{cardinality} on every kind would subsume @code{list}, and @code{list} is a
documented key, so removing it would cost a format number and a migration for a
tidier vocabulary and nothing else. It stays. This is written down so the next
person to notice argues with the reasoning rather than rediscovering the wart.

@node Filters
@chapter Filters

@cindex filter expressions
Every query command accepts the same expression grammar. Clauses are separated
by commas and combined with AND; alternatives within a clause are separated by
@samp{|}.

@example
cairn list --filter 'status=doing'
cairn list --filter 'priority=p0|p1,category!=done'
cairn list --filter 'labels~auth'
cairn list --filter 'milestone='
cairn list --filter 'updated<2026-06-01'
@end example

@noindent
The operators are:

@table @code
@item =
Equal, case-insensitively. For list fields, membership.
@item !=
Not equal.
@item ~
Contains, case-insensitively.
@item !~
Does not contain.
@item > >= < <=
Ordered comparison, numeric when both sides parse as numbers and lexical
otherwise --- which is why ISO dates compare correctly.
@end table

@cindex empty values
An empty value tests for absence: @samp{milestone=} matches items with no
milestone, and @samp{milestone!=} matches items that have one.

Besides the item's own fields, several pseudo-fields are available:
@code{category} resolves through the status table, so @samp{category=active}
selects whatever your active statuses happen to be called; @code{blocked} and
@code{ready} resolve through the dependency graph; @code{blockers} lists the
unfinished dependencies; and @code{body} matches the Markdown below the
frontmatter, which makes @samp{body~oauth} a full-text search.

@cindex sorting
@option{--sort} takes a comma-separated list of fields, each optionally
prefixed with @samp{-} for descending order. @code{status} sorts by configured
order, @code{milestone} by due date, and enum fields by declared value order.
Empty values sort last.

@node Working the backlog
@chapter Working the backlog

@cindex next
@cindex claiming
@cindex agents
A backlog is only useful if it can answer @dfn{what should I do now?}, and
answering that from a listing requires knowing the schema, composing a filter,
and resolving dependencies by hand. Two commands do it directly.

@example
$ cairn next
ID    STATUS       MILESTONE  BLOCKED BY  TITLE
0001  in progress  v0.1                   Publish to crates.io
0005  planned      v1.0                   Freeze the item file format
@end example

@command{cairn next} lists work that is @dfn{ready}: not finished, and with no
unfinished dependencies. Work already under way is ranked first, on the grounds
that the most useful next action is usually finishing something. @option{--blocked}
includes blocked work, annotated with what blocks it.

@example
$ cairn claim --next
claimed 0001  Publish to crates.io
  claude . doing . cairn/items/0001-publish-to-crates-io.md
@end example

@command{cairn claim} records who is doing what, in the item, where everybody
can see it and git can merge it. It refuses an item somebody else holds, and one
that is blocked, unless @option{--force} is given. @command{cairn release} hands
an item back. @option{--next} picks and claims the top-ranked unclaimed item in
one step.

@cindex CAIRN_USER
Identity is @env{CAIRN_USER} if set, otherwise the repository's
@code{user.name}, otherwise the login name --- so an agent can identify itself
without altering anybody's git configuration.

@cindex coordination
This is the whole coordination mechanism. There is no lock server and no
daemon: a claim is a field in a file. Two workers on two branches merge like
any other change, and @command{cairn check} catches what the merge could not.

@section Serving the backlog over MCP

@cindex Model Context Protocol
@cindex MCP
@command{cairn mcp} speaks the Model Context Protocol on standard input and
output, exposing the backlog as tools: @code{get_schema}, @code{next_items},
@code{list_items}, @code{search_items}, @code{show_item}, @code{claim_item},
@code{create_item}, @code{update_item}, @code{add_note}, @code{close_item} and
@code{check}.

@code{add_note} appends to an item's body and @code{update_item} replaces it.
The distinction is deliberate: a caller recording why it decided something must
not be able to erase what came before by getting one field wrong.

@example
cairn mcp --config
@end example

@noindent
prints the configuration a client needs. Tools are preferable to written
instructions for the same reason a type is preferable to a comment: they are
checked. A rejected write returns the reason and the permitted values, so the
caller can correct itself rather than guess.

Every item returned by these tools carries @code{blocked}, @code{ready} and
@code{blockers} alongside its fields, because dependency state cannot be
computed from one item and is the thing a caller most needs.

Two invariants hold the transport together: standard output carries protocol
and nothing else, with every diagnostic on standard error; and a tool that
fails reports the failure in its result rather than as a protocol error, so the
conversation continues.

@node Interchange
@chapter Interchange

@cindex import
@cindex export
@cindex adapters
One documented format sits between cairn and every tracker it will ever talk
to. Adapters produce it or consume it; the core knows nothing about any
particular forge. A tracker cairn has never heard of is one @command{jq} script
away from working, and no proprietary service is built into the program.

@example
cairn export --to json --output backlog.json
cairn import --from json backlog.json
cairn import --from github --repo owner/name
@end example

@section The document

A JSON object with four keys: @code{cairn}, the format version;
@code{exported}, the date; @code{project}; @code{schema}, the exporting
project's types, statuses, fields and milestones; and @code{items}. Each item
carries its fields, its Markdown body, its dependencies, and its
@code{category}.

@cindex category, in interchange
That last one is load-bearing. Statuses are named per project, so an importer
cannot match them across a boundary --- one project's @code{shipped} is
another's @code{done} is another's @code{closed}. Categories are fixed, so an
item arriving from anywhere can always be placed even when its status name means
nothing locally.

The importer is liberal in what it accepts: a full document, a bare array of
items, or a single item. Every key is optional. An adapter written in an
afternoon should still import as far as it can.

@section Mapping

Resolution runs in order of confidence: an explicit @option{--map}, then an
exact name match, then the category.

@example
cairn import --from json in.json     --map status:open=icebox     --map type:enhancement=feature     --map milestone:Backlog=later
@end example

@noindent
@option{--create-milestones} creates milestones the document mentions but the
project does not have. Anything that still cannot be placed --- an undeclared
field, a type with no local equivalent --- is reported as a warning rather than
dropped in silence. @option{--dry-run} reports without writing.

@section Closing the issue it came from

@cindex inbox, GitHub as
@option{--close} closes each imported issue upstream, with a comment naming the
item it became and where that item lives.

@example
cairn import --from github --repo owner/name --close
@end example

The repository is the system of record and the tracker is an inbox. If both stay
open the same work exists in two places and diverges from the moment it is
imported. This is the whole of what two-way synchronisation was meant to solve,
and it goes one direction only: no identifier mapping, no reconciliation, no
update loop.

Issues are closed only after every item is safely on disk. Closing one that
pointed at something which failed to save would send its reporter to a dead
link.

@section Repeating an import

@cindex source
@cindex idempotence
Every imported item records where it came from in a @code{source} field, of the
form @samp{github:owner/repo#12}. Import matches on it, so running the same
import twice leaves the backlog unchanged rather than doubling it.
@option{--update} refreshes matched items from the document instead of skipping
them.

@section GitHub

@command{cairn import --from github} shells out to the @command{gh}
command-line tool rather than speaking HTTP itself. There is no token handling
inside cairn and no second place for credentials to live, and enterprise hosts
work because @command{gh} is already configured for them. Install it from
@url{https://cli.github.com} and run @command{gh auth login} first.

@node Stability
@chapter Stability

@cindex stability
@cindex scripting
The file format has a version, a policy and a corpus that pins it. It was for a
while the only part of cairn that had promised anybody anything, which is a poor
position for a tool people are invited to script against: output that is an
accident of implementation cannot be depended on, and everybody sensible
notices.

This chapter says what may change and what may not. The rules are the ones the
format already uses: within a major version, things may be added and may not be
removed or renamed.

@section What is promised

@table @asis
@item The item format
Versioned. Additive in a minor release; anything else needs a new format number
and a migration. @xref{Compatibility}.

@item JSON output
@cindex --json
@command{list}, @command{next}, @command{search}, @command{show},
@command{log} and @command{config} take @option{--json}. Fields may be added.
Within a major version, a documented field is never removed, never renamed, and
never changes type.

Every item in JSON carries @code{id}, @code{title}, @code{type},
@code{status}, @code{category}, @code{milestone}, @code{assignee},
@code{labels}, @code{depends_on}, @code{created}, @code{updated},
@code{source}, @code{ref} and @code{fields}. @command{show} adds @code{body};
@command{next} adds @code{blockers} and @code{ready}.

@item Identifiers only
@cindex --ids
@option{--ids} prints one identifier per line, formatted as the project
configures. Nothing else is on those lines, ever.

@item Tab-separated output
@cindex --plain
@option{--plain} prints machine values, tab-separated, with no header and no
colour --- @code{doing}, not @samp{in progress}. On @command{list} the columns
are whatever @option{--columns} says, defaulting to the project's configured
columns; on @command{next} and @command{search} they are fixed.

@item Counts
@option{--count} prints one integer and nothing else.

@item The filter grammar
@cindex filter grammar
Field names, operators and the pseudo-fields @code{category}, @code{blocked},
@code{ready} and @code{blockers}. Additive only: an expression that works today
works in every later release of this major version. @xref{Filters}.

@item Exit codes
@cindex exit codes
@table @code
@item 0
The command did what was asked.
@item 1
The operation failed, or found problems it was asked to look for --- a
@command{check} that found errors, a confirmation that was declined.
@item 2
The command line was wrong. This is clap's, and it is never used for anything
else.
@end table

@item MCP tool names and schemas
@cindex Model Context Protocol
The eleven tools, their names and their arguments. Arguments may be added.
Removing a tool, or an argument, is a major release.

@item The interchange document
Carries its own version. @xref{Interchange}.
@end table

@section What is deliberately not promised

@cindex unstable surfaces
This list matters as much as the one above, because it is how the tool stays
improvable.

@itemize @bullet
@item
The exact wording of anything written for a person to read, including error
messages. Scripts must not match on them; @option{--json} exists so they do not
have to.

@item
Colour, table widths, column alignment, and whether a column appears at all in
the human table --- empty ones are dropped.

@item
The order of anything not documented as ordered. @option{--sort} is the way to
depend on an order.

@item
Anything printed on standard error while standard output carries the answer.
Notes, warnings and progress live there precisely so they can change.

@item
The wording and layout of the generated @file{ROADMAP.md} and of
@command{cairn agent}. Both are generated from the schema and both will improve.
@end itemize

@section What this costs

@cindex adding a field
Being specific has a price worth stating plainly: a field named here cannot be
withdrawn without a major release, so adding one is a decision rather than a
convenience. That is the intended effect. @file{tests/stability.rs} asserts that
every documented field is present, so removing one fails the build rather than
somebody's script.

@section Translation

@cindex translation
@cindex internationalisation
@cindex i18n
cairn is not translated, and will not be before 1.0. That is a decision rather
than an oversight, so here is the reasoning and what would reverse it.

A GNU-shaped project is normally translatable, and cairn borrows its manners
from that tradition. The gap is real. But most of what cairn prints is not
labels; it is prose. A diagnostic names a file and a line and says what would
have worked. An agent instruction block is generated from the project's own
schema. An error message's whole job is to be precise about what went wrong.

Translating those well is a sustained editorial commitment rather than a one-off
extraction, and a half-translated diagnostic is worse than an English one,
because it can no longer be searched for --- which is how people actually
resolve errors. A translated error is also harder for a maintainer to act on
when it arrives in a bug report.

So the cost here is maintenance, not extraction, and it is not a cost a project
should take on before it has anybody to bear it.

@subsection What would change this

A contributor volunteering to own a language --- not to produce one
translation, but to keep it current as messages change. On that, the answer
becomes yes and the work is a message catalogue plus @emph{one complete}
language rather than several partial ones, for the reason above.

Until then, English messages sit alongside the English that git, cargo and the
compiler already print to the same person.

@node Agents and ownership
@chapter Agents and ownership

@cindex agents
@cindex permissions
The schema tells a model exactly which statuses, types and fields exist, and
that closed vocabulary is the most useful thing cairn does for one. It says
what @emph{exists}. This chapter is about what an agent may @emph{touch}, and
about telling apart who is working from who is answerable.

@section What an agent may do

@vindex agent
@example
[[field]]
name  = "priority"
agent = "read-only"      # may read it, may not set it

[[status]]
name  = "done"
agent = "propose"        # may ask for it; a person decides
@end example

@noindent
Both default to unrestricted, so a project that says nothing behaves as it
always has.

A refusal says what to do instead, because an agent that is only told no learns
nothing:

@example
cairn: `claude` may read `priority` but not set it, by this project's
cairn.toml. Say what you believe in a note on the item instead, and a
person can make the change.
@end example

@noindent
@code{propose} needs somewhere for the proposal to go, and the item's body is
already where every other reason lives. An agent that believes something is
finished writes down why, and a person moves it.

@command{cairn agent} carries these restrictions into the instruction block, so
a model reads them rather than discovering them by being refused.

@section What an agent is, and is not, to cairn

@cindex agents
cairn treats an agent as @emph{a user who happens to be a program}. That premise
produced most of what is here: the schema first, @command{next} as the
primitive, claims against duplicate work, permissions that are enforced, notes
that append rather than replace, and this very block generated from the real
schema so it cannot drift.

The premise is right about writing and wrong in three specific ways. They are
written down because every decision about agents turns on one of them, and
because the refusals they imply are each individually tempting.

@subsection A person's memory outlives the session; an agent's does not

The backlog is not the agent's record. It is its memory.

That is why filing a duplicate is not an unlucky mistake but the characteristic
failure of an agent using this tool, and why @command{cairn new} reports a near
duplicate rather than staying silent. It is why a reason for handing work back
has to be @emph{in the item} --- a person who releases something says so in
passing, in a message or at a standup; an agent has no passing. And it is why
@option{--since} exists, because a returning caller had one option, which was to
read everything again.

@subsection A person's attention is free; an agent's is metered

Every character returned is charged to the caller. A person skimming a table
pays nothing for the columns they ignore.

That is why a read path returning everything is not a minor inefficiency but a
design error, and why @code{fields} exists on the read tools. Twenty-two keys
and six hundred characters an item, for a question that needed five of them.

@subsection A person notices they have stopped; an agent ceases to exist

Every operation that takes a lock on @emph{intent} assumes the holder comes
back. A session is killed or hits a limit, and it does not.

That is why a claim records @code{claimed}, and why stale means visible and
never revoked.

@section What follows from this, including what is refused

@cindex refusals
The same three say what @emph{not} to build, which is the more useful half.

@table @strong
@item No parallel agent API
The value is that agents and people share one backlog. The moment there is an
agent-shaped view and a human-shaped view they drift, and the shared artefact
stops being shared. Every tool the protocol server offers is a command a person
also has.

@item No memory or scratchpad store
The items are the memory. Anything else is the @file{TODO} file this very block
tells an agent not to write.

@item No leases and no heartbeats
That is a distributed-systems answer to a filesystem problem. It would put state
in the repository a person cannot read, and it would require something to be
running --- which is precisely what cannot be assumed about the thing that
stopped.

@item Nothing that calls a model
cairn is read and written by programs that think. It does not think. A tool that
summarised a backlog would be wrong occasionally and unfalsifiably, and the
whole value here is that the file says what it says.
@end table

@noindent
None of this is in the specification, and it should not be: §7 is right that a
reader of items needs to know nothing about any of it. This is the tool's
posture, not the format.

@section What this is, exactly

@cindex guard rail
@strong{A guard rail, not a security boundary.}

The Model Context Protocol server knows who is calling and refuses. A command
line cannot: an agent with a shell runs @command{cairn set} and nothing here
sees it. Nothing detects an agent --- a program that wants to be treated as a
person simply is one.

@cindex transport
What makes a caller an agent over MCP is the transport, not what it says about
itself. Arriving on that connection is enough; @code{clientInfo.name} only
settles which agent, and a client that never sends one is still an agent called
@code{mcp}. This was worth getting right: while the identity came from
@code{clientInfo.name} alone, a client that called a tool before
@code{initialize} was not an agent as far as the restrictions were concerned,
and could set a field the schema reserved.

Saying otherwise would be the first dishonest sentence in this manual. The
comparison is a git hook: useful, conventional, and not a lock.

What it buys is worth having regardless. A project that can say @emph{agents may
set status and add notes, and may not change priority or acceptance criteria} is
one somebody will point an agent at, and that is worth more than any feature.

@section Proposing a change somebody else decides

@cindex propose
@cindex proposals
@vindex agent
A field or status declared @code{agent = "propose"} refuses an agent's write.
For a long time the refusal ended in advice to write a note --- and then the
proposal was prose in a body, and a person wanting to review proposals had to
read every note in the backlog to find them. The hard half was built; the place
for the answer to go was missing.

@example
$ cairn propose 12 priority=p0 --why "It blocks 0067, which blocks the release."
proposed 0012  priority: p2 -> p0
a person decides --- `cairn proposals` lists what is waiting

$ cairn proposals
0012  priority: p2 -> p0   claude, 2026-09-08
      It blocks 0067, which blocks the release.

$ cairn proposals --accept 12
accepted 0012  priority = p0
@end example

A proposal is a note with structure: the field, the value asked for, the value
now, who asked, when, and why. It is in the body because that is where every
other reason in this project lives, and because several proposals on one field
is a conversation rather than a value --- one @code{proposed_priority} would be
overwritten by the second argument. Accepting records that it was accepted,
rather than letting the change look as though it was always so.

The value is checked against the schema; the @emph{permission} is not, since
being refused the write is the reason the command exists.

@quotation
This is not an approval workflow. Nothing queues, nothing blocks, nothing
notifies. A proposal is a note somebody can find, and accepting one is a normal
write with a record of where it came from.
@end quotation

@section Types that group work

@cindex groups
@cindex container
@vindex groups
Some types are not kinds of work. A milestone is what work is filed @emph{under}:
absent from @command{cairn next}, absent from the board, absent from an ordinary
listing, and carrying the progress of everything filed under it rather than any
of its own.

A type says so:

@example
[[type]]
name = "milestone"
groups = "one"              # work belongs to at most one of these
inverse = "scheduled"
@end example

@noindent
That declaration is the whole of it. It creates the field that names one --- a
type called @code{milestone} gives items a @code{milestone:} key --- addressed
by the target's @code{key}, rolling up progress, refusing a cycle. There is
nothing else to declare and so nothing to get wrong.

@code{groups = "many"} gives a type whose field takes several: an epic, a theme,
a workstream. Work ships in exactly one release and may serve several efforts,
and that difference is a fact about the type rather than a second kind of
relationship.

@subsection The key comes from the title

@cindex key, derived
The field is addressed by @code{key}, so an item of a grouping type with no key
is a heading nothing can be filed under. @command{cairn new} therefore gives one
a key derived from its title:

@example
cairn new "v1.0" -t milestone     # key: v1.0
cairn new "Usable in anger" -t milestone
                                  # key: usable-in-anger
@end example

@noindent
Deliberately not the filename slug. A filename may lose whatever punctuation it
likes because nobody types it; a key is exactly what somebody types, so
@code{v1.0} has to survive the trip from the title.

Two titles cannot become a key, and both are refused rather than quietly
skipped, since an item of a grouping type without one is useless. A title that
reads as an identifier --- @code{0042} --- would make a reference to it
ambiguous. A title yielding a key another item of that type already answers to
would make two items answer to one name. In either case pass @option{--set
key=@dots{}}.

@command{cairn check} reports an item of a grouping type carrying no key, which
is how one that predates this, or that arrived through @command{cairn import},
gets found. Until it did, the only symptom was every attempt to file work under
it failing --- and the message said the milestone did not exist, which was the
opposite of what was wrong.

@subsection Why it is on the type

@cindex format 3
Until format 3 this was derived, and derived from the wrong end: a type was a
container because some @code{[[field]]} named it as a @code{target}. You could
not read it off the type; adding a field changed another type's behaviour; and
saying it took eleven lines that said "milestone" four times.

It also produced a defect worth remembering. @command{cairn board --group-by
milestone} silently dropped every scheduled item, because the code computing
@emph{is this a container} ran against a list the containers had already been
removed from. A declared flag cannot be computed wrongly.

@subsection What is left of ref fields

@code{kind = "ref"} remains, for a genuine general reference --- a @code{related}
field pointing at anything at all. It keeps @code{target}, @code{by},
@code{cardinality}, @code{acyclic}, @code{rollup} and @code{inverse}, and it is
now rare rather than the way the commonest thing in the schema was spelled.

@code{depends_on} is unchanged and undeclarable: sequencing is universal, and
asking every project to declare it would be asking for a decision nobody has.

@section A claim is a promise somebody might not keep

@cindex claim
@cindex stale
@vindex claimed
@command{cairn claim} takes a lock on @emph{intent}, and it was the only lock
here with nothing recording when it was taken. A person who stops working
notices. An agent does not stop --- it ceases to exist, when a session is killed
or hits a limit --- and the item it held stayed @code{doing} for ever: invisible
to @command{cairn next} because it was claimed, and invisible to a person
because nothing listed it.

A claim now writes @code{claimed}, a date, beside @code{assignee}. It is its own
key because @code{updated} means something else and every other edit moves it.
Handing the item back or finishing it clears it.

@example
claim_stale_after = 3     # in [project], days
@end example

@noindent
With that set, a claim untouched for longer is @dfn{stale}. @command{cairn next}
offers it back, saying who holds it and for how long; @code{stale} and
@code{held_days} work in any filter and as columns; and @command{cairn claim}
will take one over without @option{--force}, saying whose it was.

@quotation
@strong{Stale means visible, never revoked.} Nothing is released automatically.
Taking work away from somebody slow is worse than leaving it held, and cairn
cannot tell the two apart --- only a person can.
@end quotation

Absent, nothing is ever stale and nothing about the tool changes. A project
where a claim means an afternoon and one where it means a quarter are both real,
and neither is cairn's to guess.

@section Handing work back

@cindex release
@command{cairn release --reason} appends a dated note in the releaser's name,
and @command{cairn claim} shows the most recent one to whoever takes the item
next.

The reason is the most valuable thing somebody handing work back knows, and it
used to evaporate: an agent would spend an hour discovering an approach does not
work, release, and the next one would walk the same dead end. A person who
releases something usually says so in passing --- in a message, at a standup, to
whoever asks. An agent has no passing. If it is not in the item, it did not
happen.

It is a note rather than a field on purpose. Three attempts on a hard item is a
history, and a history is what a body is for; one @code{released_because} would
be overwritten by the second attempt.

@section Asking for less

@cindex context
@cindex fields
Every read over the protocol used to return everything cairn knows: twenty-two
keys, about six hundred characters an item. A caller asking @emph{what should I
do next} needs five of them, and across fifty items that is most of a context
window spent on an answer nobody wanted.

@example
@{"name": "next_items", "arguments": @{"fields": ["id", "title", "priority", "blocked"]@}@}
@end example

@noindent
A field an item does not carry comes back @code{null} rather than failing --- a
milestone has no priority, and asking for one across a mixed list is a
reasonable question. A name the schema does not declare is an error saying what
is available, because silence there would look like an item without a value.
@code{id} is always included: a result nothing can be acted on is worth less
than the bytes it took.

Absent, the shape is exactly what it was. Changing a default is how every
consumer breaks at once.

A person skimming a table pays nothing for the columns they ignore. This is the
boundary where they do, which is the whole reason it is here and not on the
command line, where @option{--columns} has always done the same job.

@section What changed since I last looked

@cindex since
@example
cairn list --since 2026-09-01
cairn search parser --since 2026-09-01
@end example

@noindent
Items whose @code{updated} falls on or after a date, on @command{list},
@command{search} and the read tools. A returning caller had one option, which
was to read everything again.

@quotation
@strong{This is not an audit trail.} @code{updated} moves when a field changes
and not when only a body does, and it says nothing about *what* changed. It is a
filter for "look at these again". @command{cairn log} is the audit trail, and it
works because git is underneath it.
@end quotation

Day granularity, because that is all @code{updated} carries. Anything finer
would be a lie, and pretending otherwise would push cairn towards timestamps in
frontmatter that a person has to read.

@section Filing the same thing twice

@cindex duplicates
@command{cairn new} reports items whose titles look like the one being filed,
and creates it anyway:

@example
$ cairn new "Rate limit the API"
note: 0004 "Rate-limit the public API" looks similar --- check before filing another
created 0042  Rate limit the API
@end example

Reported, never refused. Two items genuinely called the same thing is a real
situation, and a tool that refused would teach people to mangle titles to get
past it, which is worse than a duplicate.

This is aimed at agents in particular. A person filing the same bug twice
usually half-remembers doing it; an agent starts every session cold, so the
backlog is not its record but its memory. Over the protocol the warning is in
the tool @emph{result} rather than on standard error, because a result is what a
model reads.

@section Who is working, and who is answerable

@vindex owner
@vindex created_by
@code{assignee} is who is doing the work. @code{owner} is who is answerable for
it. With people the two are usually the same, which is why one field served for
so long; with an agent working and a person owning they are not, and claiming
used to overwrite the record of who cared.

@command{cairn claim} sets @code{assignee} and leaves @code{owner} alone.

@code{created_by} records what made an item, and only when that was an agent. A
person filing something is the ordinary case and needs no annotation: writing
two lines into every item forever would cost the readability that makes this
format worth having, for a signal only the other kind carries.

So the absence of @code{created_by} means a person, and the question worth
asking as the proportion written by agents rises is the pair --- something an
agent made, that no person has yet taken responsibility for:

@example
cairn list --filter 'created_by!=,owner='
@end example

@noindent
@option{--mine} on @command{cairn next} means assigned to you, owned by you, or
taken by nobody.

@node Fields that name items
@chapter Fields that name items

@cindex ref
@cindex reference
@cindex key
Most fields describe an item. A @dfn{reference} names another one.

The difference is whether the value has its own existence. @code{priority = p0}
is a string: there is nothing more to know about @code{p0}. A milestone is not a
string --- it has a due date, a reason for that date, and a history of the date
moving --- and a field whose values are milestones is naming things rather than
describing this one.

@example
[[field]]
name        = "milestone"
kind        = "ref"
target      = "milestone"    # values name an item of this type
cardinality = "one"          # or "many"
by          = "key"          # or "id"
acyclic     = true           # refuse a value that closes a cycle
rollup      = true           # contribute to what is named
inverse     = "scheduled"    # what the question is called backwards
@end example

@section Why there is one mechanism and not two

@cindex depends_on
@command{depends_on} was hardcoded for as long as cairn had only one kind of
edge, and it was the single place where ``the schema is yours'' was untrue: the
one relationship a project could not declare, redefine, or give a sibling.

The obvious repair was a second table for declarable edges, and it would have
been a mistake. A reference needs a target type, a cycle check, an inverse and a
rollup, which is the entire feature set an edge needs. Two tables means building
that twice, documenting it twice, and handing a model two vocabularies for one
idea.

They differ in exactly two ways: how many values are allowed, and how a value
names its target. So there is one table, and it is @code{[[field]]}.
@code{depends_on} is now described as what it always was --- a many-valued
reference to any item, addressed by identifier, acyclic --- and
@command{cairn config --json} reports it alongside the fields a project
declares.

@section Keys

@vindex key
@example
---
id: 42
key: v0.1
title: Usable in anger
type: milestone
---
@end example

@noindent
A key is what keeps @code{milestone: v0.1} readable in a file instead of
becoming @code{milestone: 42}. That readability is a large part of why this
format is worth having, and it would be a poor trade for internal tidiness.

@cindex identity
A key is @emph{not} an identity. @code{id} is unique across the project, is what
identity means, and does not change. A key is a handle: unique only among items
of one type, chosen rather than allocated, and permitted to change. The
precedent is already in the schema one level down --- a status has a @code{name}
for machines and a @code{label} for people.

Two rules keep it unambiguous.

A key must not read as an identifier under the project's @code{id_format}
(@pxref{Identifiers}). Otherwise @code{milestone: 0042} could name either a key
or a number depending on what happened to exist, and two spellings must never be
able to mean different items. cairn refuses such a key when it is set.

A key-addressed reference resolves @emph{only} by key --- never by identifier
and never by title. A value that names nothing is refused, and the refusal lists
what would have worked, which is most of what somebody who mistyped a milestone
wants.

@subsection Changing one

@cindex renaming a key
A key is what other items call this one, so changing it is a rename rather than
an assignment. cairn rewrites every reference under the same lock and says which
items it touched:

@example
$ cairn set 1 key=v2.0
updated 0001  Version one
  also 0002
  also 0003
@end example

@noindent
Leaving them behind would orphan every reference silently, which is the failure
this exists to prevent. @command{renumber} already does exactly this when an
identifier moves; this is the same job one level up.

@section Containers

@cindex container
A type named as a specific @code{target} --- not @code{*} --- is a
@dfn{container}: the thing work belongs to rather than work itself. Items of a
container type do not appear in @command{cairn next} or on the board.

This is derived rather than declared, so there is nothing to configure and
nothing to forget. It is also why @code{target = "*"} exists: @code{depends_on}
can name any item without turning every type into a container and emptying
@command{cairn next}.

It settles a second problem too. Containers naturally depend on one another ---
a roadmap is a sequence --- and without this they would read as blocked work and
crowd out the real thing.

@section Composition

@cindex composition
@cindex part_of
@cindex hierarchy
Large work is made of small work, and @code{part_of} is how an item says which
larger effort it belongs to. @command{cairn init} scaffolds it, so a new project
composes without configuring anything.

@example
cairn new "Ship OAuth"
cairn new "Token endpoint"
cairn set 2 part_of=1
@end example

@noindent
It is scaffolded rather than built in. Hardcoding a second relationship would
re-create the problem declarable fields exist to remove, and composition is
genuinely optional in a way ordering is not --- many backlogs are flat. An
existing project adds the four lines from @file{cairn.toml} and has it.

@subsection Why there is no parent field

@cindex parent
@code{part_of} is many-valued, and that is the point. An item belongs to the
authentication work and to the quarter's security push at once, and a tree makes
you choose one and then makes you wrong later.

There is a second reason specific to files in a repository. A scalar has no
correct merge: when two branches file the same item under different parents, one
of them simply loses and nobody can tell which was meant. A set has an obvious
answer --- keep both --- so the disagreement becomes a merge somebody can
finish rather than a decision the format already made for them.

That is not the same as merging cleanly, and it is worth being exact. Two
branches appending to the same sequence still produce a textual conflict today,
because git merges text; @code{depends_on} has always behaved this way.
Resolving such a conflict by union is a job for the merge driver, and it is not
done yet.

@subsection How deep

@cindex depth
Unbounded. A limit is a decision that will be wrong for somebody.

Past about four levels @command{cairn check} says so, as a warning rather than
an error, because a hierarchy that deep is usually a taxonomy where a plan was
wanted. It is a remark, not a rule.

@subsection Asking about position

@cindex progress
@cindex descendants
@cindex depth
@cindex leaf
Where an item sits is derived from the graph rather than declared on the item.
Nothing is stored, so nothing can go stale.

@table @code
@item contains
What is directly beneath it.
@item descendants
How many items are beneath it at any depth.
@item progress
What proportion of those are finished. Absent for an item containing nothing:
a leaf has no progress to report, and reporting zero would put every ordinary
item at the bottom of @code{--sort progress}.
@item depth
How far below a root it sits.
@item leaf
Whether anything is beneath it.
@end table

@example
cairn list --filter 'depth=0,progress<50'      # big things that are behind
cairn list --filter 'leaf=true,ready=true'     # work that can start today
@end example

@noindent
This is why there is no @code{scale} field. A declared size is a claim that goes
stale --- something filed as large turns out to be an afternoon, and nobody
returns to correct the label --- while @code{descendants} cannot be wrong.

A field marked @code{rollup} contributes to all of this, whatever its
cardinality or addressing, so there is one derivation however many fields point
in rather than a mechanism for each.

@subsection Filing stays free

@command{cairn new} takes a title and nothing else, always. Structure is added
afterwards, by a second command, and never demanded at the moment somebody is
trying to write something down.

@section Level names

@cindex initiative
@cindex epic
cairn ships no @code{initiative}, no @code{epic}, no @code{story}. A project
that wants them declares them:

@example
[[type]]
name = "initiative"
@end example

@noindent
That is the whole mechanism, and cairn has no opinion about it.

Naming a level is where tools in this category begin to mislead their users. The
moment @code{epic} exists in the program, people argue about whether something
is an epic --- an argument with no answer in it, because levels are not facts
about work but a vocabulary imposed on it, and the vocabulary fits some projects
and not others. Then the tool's word and the team's word diverge and everybody
carries a translation table.

Levels are also unstable in a way the tool cannot see. Something filed as an
epic turns out to be an afternoon; something filed as a task grows six items
beneath it. The label does not update, and within a quarter the field is
decoration.

None of it is necessary. Size is a fact about the graph: an item with fourteen
things @code{part_of} it is observably large, and that cannot go stale.

@subsection What would reverse this

A behaviour that genuinely needs a declared level rather than a derived one ---
a query, a view, a rollup that cannot be written against @code{part_of}. That
would be a fact rather than a preference, and it would settle the question.

Wanting the word is not that. The word is available; it is a type name, and it
belongs to the project rather than to this program.

@section What this does not change

Nothing on disk. @code{kind = "ref"} and its properties are new optional keys in
the configuration, @code{key} is a new optional key in an item, and
@code{depends_on} is declared as exactly what it already meant. A project that
declares nothing behaves as it did before, and the format is still 1.

@node Acceptance criteria
@chapter Acceptance criteria

@cindex acceptance criteria
@cindex checkboxes
@cindex definition of done
An item's body may state how you will know it is finished:

@example
## Acceptance criteria

- [x] Follows renames, so a retitled item keeps its history
- [ ] Works in a shallow clone, and says what it could not see
@end example

cairn reads those boxes, and writes one when you say so. Ticking a criterion is
a judgement, and the judgement stays with the person or the agent answering for
the work --- but recording it is clerical, and for a while cairn offered no way
to do the clerical part. @command{set} takes fields, @command{note} appends to
the body, @command{edit} wants a terminal. So the most-touched part of an item
in a working loop was the one part with no command, and what people did instead
was rewrite the Markdown from outside cairn. One of them truncated an item to
zero bytes doing it.

@section What reads them

@table @asis
@item @command{cairn close}
Says what is still unticked, at the moment somebody declares the work done
against what they themselves wrote down that done would mean. It never refuses:
a criterion can stop applying, and a tool that blocked here would teach people
to tick boxes rather than to say what is true.

@item @command{cairn show}
Reports the count, and marks it when every box is ticked.
@option{--criteria} prints them in full, numbered the way @command{cairn tick}
numbers them; with @option{--json} the same list comes out machine-readable.

@item Filters
@code{criteria}, @code{criteria_done} and @code{criteria_met} are derived from
the body, so they cannot go stale.

@example
cairn list -A --filter 'category=done,criteria_met=false'
@end example

@code{criteria_met} exists rather than leaving you to write
@code{criteria_done < criteria}, which the grammar cannot express: a comparison
is always against a literal.

@item The Model Context Protocol server
@code{close_item} reports what remains, so an agent closing something is told
what it said done would mean. Again without refusing --- the agent may be right
and the criteria stale.
@end table

@section What writes them

@cindex ticking criteria
@table @asis
@item @command{cairn tick}, @command{cairn untick}
@example
cairn show 12 --criteria    # 2 of 5 ticked
cairn tick 12 3             # the third
cairn tick 12 1 2 5
cairn tick 12 --all
cairn untick 12 3
@end example

The numbers count from 1 and are the ones @command{cairn show --criteria}
prints; both sides read one parser, so they cannot come to disagree about
whether an indented box is a criterion.

Only the box is rewritten. Indentation, any brackets inside the text, and
trailing notes all survive, because the criterion is the sentence that says what
done means and moving it is the failure the command exists to prevent.

Naming a criterion that is not there refuses the whole command rather than
applying the numbers that were valid. Ticking what is already ticked is not an
error and writes nothing at all: that is what a retried script does, and a tool
that failed there would make the retry the dangerous path.

@item The Model Context Protocol server
@code{tick_criteria} takes the same numbers @code{show_item} reports, for the
same reason: an agent has no editor to fall back on, and without this the only
way for one to record a criterion as met was to rewrite the body --- the one
operation in the protocol that can destroy an item.
@end table

@section What is not a criterion

@cindex placeholder
A box with nothing written after it is a placeholder, not a criterion. The type
template cairn ships ends with a bare @code{- [ ]} prompting the author to write
one, so counting it would make every item ever created report one unticked
criterion for the rest of its life --- which is the noise that gets a feature
switched off within a week.

@section An item that states none

@noindent
An item with no boxes is @emph{vacuously} complete, and nothing above says
anything about it. Most items have no criteria and must not be reported as
unfinished, or the signal is worth nothing within a week.

@section Where they live

@vindex criteria_section
By default every checkbox in a body counts, because an item that put its
criteria under a different heading still meant them. A project that keeps them
somewhere specific can say so:

@example
[project]
criteria_section = "Acceptance criteria"
@end example

@noindent
Then only boxes under a heading of that name count, matched case-insensitively
at any heading depth. A body with no such heading states no criteria --- it
does not fall back to counting everything, which would silently include the
boxes the setting exists to exclude.

@section Making it a rule

@vindex require_criteria
@example
[project]
require_criteria = true
@end example

@noindent
Then @command{cairn check} warns about a closed item whose criteria are
unticked, @option{--strict} makes that fail, and @command{cairn close} refuses
to close one in the first place --- as does the MCP @code{close_item} tool.

That refusal is the exception to the rule stated above, and it is not a
contradiction of it. The default never blocks, because a criterion can stop
applying. A project that writes @code{require_criteria} has already decided it
wants the gate, and getting it only from @command{cairn check} means getting it
after the commit that closed the item.

It is off by default, and the reason is worth stating rather than assuming. An
unticked box is not a schema violation the way an undefined status is; it is a
judgement about process. A project adopting cairn mid-life would turn this on,
receive a wall of warnings about work finished years ago, turn it off again, and
from then on it would be worth nothing. The information is actionable at the
moment of closing, which is why that is where it is reported unconditionally.

@noindent
This project has not turned it on. Its own backlog holds two hundred unticked
criteria on closed items, written before anything read them, and mass-ticking
them retrospectively would be exactly the ritual this feature exists to avoid.

@node Verifying a release
@chapter Verifying a release

@cindex verification
@cindex signatures
@cindex provenance
@cindex source tarball
A backlog is years of a project's history, and the program that reads it is
worth checking before it is trusted with them. This chapter is written for
somebody who has not done this before; each step says what it proves and what it
does not.

Every release carries, for each platform, an archive and its SHA-256 checksum,
plus @file{SHA256SUMS} listing all of them, and a source tarball.

@section What the tests prove, and what they do not

@cindex make check
@cindex make durability
Before any of that, there is the question of whether the program itself was
checked. Two levels, and the difference between them is worth stating rather
than leaving to be discovered.

@table @code
@item make check
The suite, formatting, lints, and cairn's own backlog validated against its own
schema. Every change must pass it. It does @strong{not} exercise concurrency:
both soak tests are marked ignored, so the lock, the atomic write and identifier
allocation get no contention, and the fuzz pass it runs is 600 argument vectors
rather than 20,000.

@item make durability
Everything. @code{check}, then the soak under contention, then the long fuzz,
then the second reader over every format that has ever existed. Every stage
prints the seed it used, so a failure is reproducible by passing it back.
@end table

@noindent
The short level is deliberate --- a suite nobody will wait for is a suite nobody
runs --- and the cost is that "the tests pass" means less than it sounds like.
Hence the second name.

Run @command{make durability} before tagging, and after touching any of four
things: the lock, the write path, identifier allocation, or the merge driver.
Those are where a defect costs somebody their work rather than their afternoon.

Continuous integration still runs the pieces as separate jobs, so a failure says
which one rather than that something, somewhere, went wrong.

@section What a checksum proves

@example
sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
@end example

@noindent
This proves the file arrived intact. It proves nothing about who made it: the
checksums are produced by the same workflow as the archives, so anybody able to
tamper with one could tamper with the other. It catches a truncated download and
a broken mirror, which is worth having and is not the same as trust.

@section What the attestation proves

@cindex build provenance
GitHub signs a statement saying which workflow, from which commit, produced each
file. That is the guarantee a checksum cannot give, and it needs no key of ours.

@example
gh attestation verify cairn-0.1.0-x86_64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz \
   --repo oddurs/cairn
@end example

@noindent
It says: this file came out of that repository's release workflow, at that
commit. It relies on GitHub's signing infrastructure, which is a party you are
choosing to trust --- an honest description of what it is, rather than a claim
of independence it does not have.

@section What a signature proves

@cindex GPG
When a signing key is configured, every artefact has a detached
@file{.asc} beside it.

@example
gpg --verify cairn-0.1.0-x86_64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz.asc \
             cairn-0.1.0-x86_64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz
@end example

@noindent
This is worth exactly as much as your confidence that the key is the right one,
which is why the fingerprint is published in the repository and on the website
rather than only in the release --- a fingerprint distributed alongside the
thing it signs proves nothing at all. Compare what @command{gpg} reports against
both.

@section Building it yourself

@cindex reproducing a release
The strongest check available is not to verify the archive but to reproduce it.

@example
make dist
sha256sum cairn-0.1.0.tar.gz
@end example

@noindent
The source tarball is built with @command{git archive}, which is deterministic:
file order comes from the tree and timestamps from the commit. The tarball
attached to a release and the one this command produces from the same tag are
the same bytes, and a difference is worth reporting.

Binaries are a different matter. They are not currently reproducible --- that is
a property nobody has established, and claiming it without having checked would
be worse than not offering it.

@section If a check fails

Report it (@pxref{Introduction}) with the artefact name, the release, and what
the command printed. Do not install the file.

@node Cookbook
@chapter Cookbook

@cindex cookbook
@cindex recipes
Recipes for the things people actually arrive wanting to do. Every command here
is checked by @file{tests/manual.rs}, which extracts them and refuses any the
program would reject --- the manual having twice claimed a flag that was never
implemented.

@section Adopt cairn where GitHub issues already exist

@cindex adopting cairn
@example
cairn init --name Nimbus
cairn import --from github --repo owner/name --dry-run
cairn import --from github --repo owner/name
cairn check --strict
cairn render
@end example

@noindent
The dry run first, because an import is the one operation that creates a hundred
files at once. GitHub is read through @command{gh}, which you have already
authenticated, so cairn never handles a token. Importing again later updates
rather than duplicates: provenance travels in @code{source}.

@section Triage a week's arrivals without typing an identifier twice

@example
cairn list --filter 'milestone=,category=open' --ids
cairn set --filter 'milestone=,type=bug' milestone=v0.1 priority=p1
cairn list --view triage
@end example

@noindent
The first line is the reading pass. The second changes everything it matched,
after showing you the list and asking. @xref{Filters}. If you find yourself
running the same query weekly, put it in @file{cairn.toml} as a view and give it
a name.

@section Make the roadmap unable to drift

@cindex continuous integration
@example
cairn check --strict --render
@end example

@noindent
One line in CI. @option{--strict} makes warnings fail, and @option{--render}
additionally fails when the committed @file{ROADMAP.md} does not match what the
items now say. Locally, the hooks in @file{cairn.toml} keep it current so the
check never fires. @xref{Continuous integration}.

@section Give an agent a scoped slice of the backlog

@cindex agents
@example
cairn agent --write AGENTS.md
cairn next --filter 'milestone=v0.1' --json
cairn claim --next --filter 'type=bug'
@end example

@noindent
The instruction block is generated from your schema, so it cannot describe a
workflow you do not have. An agent that speaks the Model Context Protocol should
be given @command{cairn mcp} instead, which is the same operations without any
command lines to get wrong. @xref{Working the backlog}.

@section Move a backlog between two different schemas

@example
cairn export > backlog.json
cairn import --from json backlog.json --dry-run
@end example

@noindent
The document carries the schema alongside the items, so the receiving project
can interpret status names it has never seen. Names that do not exist there are
mapped by @emph{category} --- one project's @code{shipped} is another's
@code{done} --- which is the reason categories are a fixed axis under the names
you choose. @xref{Interchange}.

@section Find everything one person is holding

@example
cairn list --assignee alice --filter 'category=active'
cairn list --filter 'assignee=,category=active'
cairn release 12
@end example

@noindent
The second line is the more useful one: work that is in progress and assigned to
nobody, which is what a stalled item looks like. An empty value means unset.

@section Recover after a bad merge

@cindex merge
@example
cairn check
cairn renumber --dry-run
cairn renumber
cairn render
@end example

@noindent
Two branches that each created an item allocate the same identifier, because no
branch can know what the other did. @command{renumber} repairs it, keeping the
identifier on the side that was already published. The dry run first, always:
this rewrites files. @xref{Identifiers}.

@section Find out what happened to an item

@example
cairn log 12
cairn log 12 --patch
cairn show 12
@end example

@noindent
Read from the repository the item lives in, and reported as field transitions
rather than as a patch. @xref{Commands}.

@node Commands
@chapter Commands

Global options apply to every subcommand.

@table @option
@item -C @var{dir}
@itemx --directory=@var{dir}
Run as if started in @var{dir}.

@item --color=@var{when}
@var{when} is @code{auto}, @code{always} or @code{never}. Under @code{auto},
colour is used when standard output is a terminal and @env{NO_COLOR} is unset.

@item --no-hooks
Do not run configured hooks. @xref{Hooks}.

@item --version
Print version, copyright and licence.

@item --bug-report
@cindex bug reports
@cindex reporting bugs
Print the details a maintainer will ask for --- version, platform, format
number, the shape of the schema, how many items there are, and how many hooks
are configured --- so they can be pasted into an issue without a conversation
first.

It deliberately reports nothing a reporter would mind publishing: no item
titles, no paths outside the project, nothing of the environment beyond the
platform. It also runs without a project, and describes a @file{cairn.toml}
that will not parse rather than failing on it, because that is exactly when
somebody reaches for it.
@end table

@cindex SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
@cindex reproducible builds
@vindex SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
If @env{SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH} is set to a Unix timestamp, cairn uses that date
instead of today's when stamping @code{created} and @code{updated}. It is the
reproducible-builds convention, and it exists here because cairn's own recorded
documentation embeds the dates of the items it creates: without it, a recording
made today differs from the one committed yesterday, and the check guarding
them fails for a reason unconnected to the program. A value that is not a
timestamp is ignored.

@section Why these commands and not others

@cindex command surface
Twenty-eight commands is a lot, and after 1.0 every one of them is a promise.
They were reviewed as a set before that happened, against one bar: a command
earns its place if removing it would make a real task @emph{harder}, not merely
different. If an existing command with a flag would do, that is the answer.

@table @asis
@item The ones a schema makes necessary
@command{close}, @command{reopen} and @command{claim} are not shorthands for
@command{set}. Closing means moving to the first status whose category is
@code{done}, which the caller would otherwise have to look up; claiming sets an
assignee and a status together, and two separate writes could interleave with
somebody else's.

@item The ones that write something @command{set} cannot
@command{note} appends to the body under a dated heading. @command{milestone}
edits @file{cairn.toml} rather than an item. @command{init} writes a schema.
@command{render} writes the roadmap. @command{renumber} repairs identifiers.

@item Three views of one query
@command{list}, @command{board} and @command{roadmap} could in principle be one
command with a flag. They are not, because they answer different questions --- 
what matches, what is in flight, and what is left before a date --- and a flag
that changes the shape of the output as much as its content is a different
command wearing a disguise. @command{next} is the fourth: it is the question an
agent asks, and making it a filter expression people must memorise would be a
real loss.

@item Two verbs for one round trip
@command{export} and @command{import} are deliberately not symmetrical.
Exporting is one thing; importing has to reconcile with what is already there,
which is where @option{--update}, @option{--dry-run} and provenance live.
Folding them together would give one command two unrelated halves.

@item Hidden, but reachable
@command{migrate} and @command{merge-driver} are not in @option{--help}.
@command{merge-driver} is called by git; @command{migrate} has nothing to
migrate until there is a format 2. Neither is a command a person needs to find,
and both are documented here.

@item Removed in the review
@option{--compact} renumbered a whole project into a gapless sequence. It moved
every identifier, which silently invalidates every commit message, pull request
and human memory that referred to one --- and cairn has no way to rewrite any of
those. That is a large, irreversible cost for a cosmetic benefit, and nothing
outside its own test ever asked for it. Removing it before 1.0 was free;
afterwards it would have been a major release.
@end table

@section One rule about flags

@cindex output shapes
Output @emph{shapes} --- @option{--json}, @option{--ids}, @option{--plain},
@option{--count} --- belong on every command that lists items, and now are on
all of them. Query @emph{controls} --- @option{--sort}, @option{--columns} ---
belong on @command{list} alone, because @command{next} and @command{search} have
opinions about order and about which columns matter, and those opinions are the
reason they exist.

Before the review @command{next} and @command{search} had @option{--json} and
@option{--ids} but not @option{--plain} or @option{--count}, for no reason
anybody could name.

@section cairn init
Writes @file{cairn.toml} and the item directory. @option{--preset} selects
@code{standard} or @code{minimal}; @option{--bare} skips the example item;
@option{--force} overwrites an existing configuration; @option{--git} adds the
git integration to a project that already exists.

@cindex adopting a schema
@option{--from} starts from another project's schema instead of a preset:

@example
cairn init --from ../other-project --name "New thing"
@end example

@noindent
Everything that describes the @emph{schema} comes across --- types, statuses,
fields, saved views, the render block, hooks --- and so do the comments, which
are half of what makes a schema legible. What does not come across is
@code{[project]}: the name and directory are this project's own, and a
description and a repository url belonged to whoever wrote them.
@code{render.link_items} is turned off with the url, so the new project does not
arrive already failing its own @command{cairn check}.

Milestones do not come across either. They are items now, and somebody else's
schedule is not a schema.

@subsection Why a copy and not an include

@cindex extends
There is no @code{extends}. An include would end @file{cairn.toml} being the
whole truth about a project: reading it would mean resolving a path that may be
outside the repository, may not exist on a clone, and may have changed since.
Somebody who clones a repository can understand its backlog, and that is worth
more than saving a copy.

The cost of copying is real --- two files that started identical drift apart ---
and it is the right cost. A schema two projects share is two projects that
cannot change independently.

@section cairn new
Creates an item. @samp{cairn new "@var{title}"} with @option{--type},
@option{--status}, @option{--milestone}, @option{--label},
@option{--assignee}, @option{--depends-on}, and
@option{--set @var{field}=@var{value}} for anything else.
@option{--body} supplies the body,
@option{--stdin} reads it from standard input, and @option{--edit} opens an
editor. @option{--quiet} prints only the new id, which is what scripts want.
Aliased as @command{cairn add}.

@section cairn list
Queries items. Convenience flags @option{--status}, @option{--type},
@option{--milestone}, @option{--label} and @option{--assignee} combine with
@option{--filter}. @option{--view} loads a saved view, @option{--sort} orders,
@option{--columns} selects columns, @option{--limit} truncates, and
@option{--all} includes finished items, which are hidden by default.

Output modes: @option{--json}, @option{--ids} (one per line, for
@command{xargs}), @option{--count}, and @option{--plain} (tab-separated,
uncoloured). Aliased as @command{cairn ls}.

@section cairn next
Ready work, ranked. @xref{Working the backlog}. @option{--limit} bounds the
list, @option{--mine} restricts to work assigned to you or to nobody,
@option{--blocked} includes blocked items, and @option{--json} and
@option{--ids} are for programs.

@section cairn search
Full-text over titles, bodies and labels, case-insensitively.
@option{--titles} restricts to titles. Aliased as @command{cairn grep}.

@section cairn claim, cairn release
Take an item or hand it back. @xref{Working the backlog}.

@section cairn mcp
Serve the backlog over the Model Context Protocol.
@option{--config} prints client configuration instead of serving.

@section cairn show
Prints one item in full. @option{--json} includes the body; @option{--raw}
prints the file unchanged; @option{--path} prints only its location.

@section cairn set
@cindex set
Changes fields on one or more items.

@example
cairn set 12 status=doing
cairn set 1 2 3 priority=p0
cairn set 12 labels+=auth labels-=stale assignee=
@end example

@noindent
Ids come first and assignments after. They share one list because an assignment
always contains @samp{=} and an id never can, so the split is unambiguous; an id
appearing after an assignment is refused rather than guessed at.

@table @option
@item --filter=@var{expr}
Change every item matching @var{expr} instead of naming ids. @xref{Filters}.

@item -y
@itemx --yes
Do not ask before a filtered change.
@end table

@example
cairn set --filter 'milestone=v0.1,status=backlog' priority=p1
@end example

@noindent
A filtered write is the dangerous one, because the person running it has not
seen the list. So the matched items are printed and the change is confirmed
before anything is touched, in the same shape @command{cairn remove} uses. A
question nobody answers --- a script with no terminal --- is answered no.

Everything is parsed before anything is written, so a typo in the last
assignment does not leave the first ones applied to half the items. The whole
operation takes one lock: a bulk edit is not a window during which the backlog
is half-changed. If a write does fail part-way, the error names the items
already written.

@section cairn note
@cindex notes
@cindex decisions, recording
Appends to an item's body, under a dated heading by default.

@example
cairn note 12 "Dropped: libguile ends the self-contained binary."
cairn note 12 --heading "Dropped, 2026-09-05" --stdin < reasoning.md
@end example

A status records what was decided; a note records why. Without somewhere to put
the reason a backlog becomes a list of things nobody remembers rejecting, and a
caller with no editor to fall back on --- an agent, a script --- cannot record
one at all.

The command is append-only by design. Replacing a body is what @command{cairn
edit} and the MCP @code{update_item} tool are for; a command whose purpose is to
add to the record should not be able to erase it. @option{--bare} omits the
heading, and @option{--stdin} reads a note longer than a line.

@section cairn tick, cairn untick
@cindex ticking criteria
Tick or untick the acceptance criteria an item states, by the numbers
@command{cairn show --criteria} prints.

@example
cairn show 12 --criteria
cairn tick 12 3
cairn tick 12 --all
cairn untick 12 3
@end example

@xref{Acceptance criteria}, for what counts as one, where they may live, and
what else reads them.

@section cairn propose, cairn proposals
@cindex propose
Ask for a change you are not permitted to make, and list what is waiting for a
person to decide.

@example
cairn propose 12 priority=p0 --why "blocks the release"
cairn proposals
@end example

@noindent
Nothing queues, nothing blocks and nothing notifies: a proposal is a note
somebody can find, and accepting one is an ordinary write with a record of where
it came from. It is the other end of the permission that refuses an agent's
write, which is why the refusal names this command.
@xref{Agents and ownership}.

@section cairn close, cairn reopen
Move items to the first @code{done} status, or back to the default open
status. @option{--status} overrides the target. Where a project sets
@code{require_criteria}, closing an item with unticked criteria is refused;
@pxref{Acceptance criteria}.

@section cairn log
@cindex history
@cindex git log
Shows how an item reached its present state, read from the repository it lives
in.

@example
$ cairn log 12
2026-09-04  oddurs   created
2026-09-05  claude   status backlog -> doing
2026-09-05  claude   note added
2026-09-11  oddurs   status doing -> done
@end example

@noindent
Changes are reported in the vocabulary of the schema rather than as a patch,
because a patch answers a different question. The @code{updated} stamp is never
reported: it changes on every write by definition, so a line for it would sit
under every real change and mean nothing.

@table @option
@item -n @var{count}
@itemx --max-count=@var{count}
Show only the most recent @var{count} revisions.

@item -p
@itemx --patch
Hand the job to @command{git log --patch}, which formats diffs better than
anything reimplemented here would.

@item --json
Machine-readable, including the commit hash and the item's path at each
revision.
@end table

@noindent
This is the only command that reads from git, and it follows the same rule as
everything else here: a project without git still works. Outside a repository,
or before an item is committed, it explains rather than fails --- an error
would make a legitimate setup look broken.

@subsection Renames, and the item it is really following

@cindex renames
Retitling an item renames its file, so the history is read with
@option{--follow}. Two things about that are worth knowing, because both were
discovered by testing rather than by reading git's documentation.

@option{--follow} and @option{--reverse} together silently truncate: on a
project with two items, asking for one returned its creating commit and nothing
after it. The log is therefore read newest-first and reversed afterwards.

More importantly, every item cairn writes has the same shape, and a fresh one
is largely boilerplate --- similar enough that git's rename detection concludes
item 2 was renamed from item 1 and follows into the wrong item's history.
Attributing one item's creation to another is worse than showing nothing,
because it looks right. So each revision is checked against the id it carries,
and the trail is cut where that changes. The identifier being stable is what
makes the correction possible.

@subsection A shallow clone

@cindex shallow clone
In a shallow clone the oldest revision on hand is a horizon rather than a
beginning. cairn checks whether that revision is one whose parents the clone
lacks, and if it is, reports it as the earliest revision present rather than as
the creation, and says on standard error what it could not see.

@section cairn edit
@cindex editor
@vindex VISUAL
@vindex EDITOR
Opens an item in @env{VISUAL}, or failing that @env{EDITOR}, then re-reads it
so a malformed edit is reported at once rather than later.

A variable that is set but empty is not a choice of editor --- shell profiles
export empty variables constantly --- so an empty @env{VISUAL} falls through to
@env{EDITOR} rather than shadowing it.

With neither set, the fallback is @command{vi} on a Unix, which POSIX requires,
and @command{notepad} on Windows, where there is no @command{vi} and
@command{notepad} is the one editor that has always shipped with the system. If
the chosen editor is not on @env{PATH}, cairn says which variable to set rather
than reporting that a process could not be spawned: the usual problem is not a
broken editor but no editor, and naming the variable is the whole of the fix.

@section cairn remove
Deletes items, asking first unless @option{--force} is given. Aliased as
@command{cairn rm}.

@section cairn board
Prints a kanban board on standard output. @option{--group-by} chooses the
column field, defaulting to @code{status}. Deliberately not an interactive
program: the output can be piped, pasted and read over a slow link.

@section cairn roadmap
Prints milestones with progress bars. @option{--items} lists their contents.

@section cairn render
Generates the roadmap file. @option{--output} redirects it, with @samp{-}
meaning standard output. @option{--check} writes nothing and exits non-zero if
the file on disk differs from what would be generated.

@section cairn export, cairn import
Move a backlog in or out. @xref{Interchange}.

@section cairn check
Validates the schema, then every item against it.

The schema first, because a configuration that misdescribes itself makes every
finding after it suspect: a status that does not declare a @code{category}, a
@code{render.group_by} or a saved view naming a field nothing declares, a view
whose filter does not parse, a @code{header} or @code{footer} that is not there,
@code{link_items} with no @code{project.url}.

Then the items: unknown statuses, types and milestones; missing required fields;
malformed enum, date, number and boolean values; undeclared fields; dangling and
circular dependencies; duplicate ids; filenames that no longer match their
titles. Diagnostics are printed as
@samp{cairn: @var{file}:@var{line}: @var{message}}. @option{--strict} makes
warnings fatal; @option{--render} additionally verifies the roadmap file.

Almost everything about the schema is a warning rather than an error, because a
saved view nobody uses must not stop @command{cairn show} from working. The
exception is a filter that does not parse, which cannot be what anybody meant.

@section cairn renumber
Repairs duplicate ids. @xref{Identifiers}.

@section cairn migrate
Brings a project to the current on-disk format, rewriting @file{cairn.toml}.
@option{--dry-run} reports what would change and writes nothing;
@option{--check} exits non-zero if the project is not already current, which is
what a continuous integration job runs. The migration to format 3 does not touch
item files. @xref{Compatibility}.

@section cairn config
Prints the resolved schema, @option{--json} for machine use, @option{--path}
for the configuration file's location.

@section cairn agent
Prints an instruction block for coding agents. @xref{Coding agents}.

@section cairn completions, cairn man
Generate a shell completion script, or manual pages.

@node Hooks
@chapter Hooks

@cindex hooks
@cindex extensibility
cairn does not embed a scripting language. It runs yours.

A hook is named in @file{cairn.toml} and run from the project root. It takes
one of two forms:

@example
[hooks]
# A string runs through the platform shell.
after-create = "cairn render -q"
after-render = "git add ROADMAP.md"

# An array is executed directly, with no shell at all.
after-change = ["python3", "scripts/notify.py"]
@end example

@cindex portable hooks
@cindex shell, platform differences
The distinction matters across platforms. A string is handed to @command{sh} on
Unix and to @command{cmd.exe} on Windows, and those disagree about almost
everything: @samp{$VAR} against @samp{%VAR%}, quoting, and which characters are
special. An array is passed straight to the operating system, so it behaves
identically everywhere. Any project expected to run on more than one platform
should use the array form and put the logic in a script.

On Windows the string form is handed to @command{cmd.exe} verbatim rather than
through Rust's argument quoting, which follows the MSVC C runtime convention
that @command{cmd.exe} does not share. A command containing quotes or
@samp{&} therefore arrives as written.

The event is supplied in the environment, and the complete item as JSON on
standard input, so a hook may be a one-line @command{sh} fragment or a program
in any language:

@table @env
@item CAIRN_EVENT
The hook name.
@item CAIRN_ROOT
Absolute path to the project root.
@item CAIRN_CONFIG
Absolute path to @file{cairn.toml}.
@item CAIRN_ITEM_ID
@itemx CAIRN_ITEM_PATH
@itemx CAIRN_ITEM_TITLE
@itemx CAIRN_ITEM_STATUS
@itemx CAIRN_ITEM_TYPE
@itemx CAIRN_ITEM_MILESTONE
@itemx CAIRN_ITEM_CATEGORY
Set for the item-scoped hooks.
@item CAIRN_RENDER_TARGET
@itemx CAIRN_ITEM_COUNT
Set for @code{after-render}.
@end table

@cindex hook failure
Every hook runs @emph{after} its change is durable, which fixes its contract:
a hook that fails prints a warning and nothing is rolled back, exactly as with
@command{git}'s @code{post-} hooks. Hooks are suppressed by @option{--no-hooks}
and by setting @env{CAIRN_NO_HOOKS}; the latter is set for the hook's own
environment, so a hook may call @command{cairn} without recursion.

@node Coding agents
@chapter Coding agents

@cindex agents
Programs that write code tend to write plans as well, and left alone they
scatter @file{TODO.md}, @file{PLAN.md} and @file{NOTES.md} through a tree, each
in a format of its own devising. The schema is the answer, and
@command{cairn agent} is how the schema reaches them.

@example
cairn agent --write AGENTS.md
@end example

This emits an instruction block --- the real statuses, the real fields, the
real milestones, read from your configuration --- between
@samp{<!-- cairn:begin -->} and @samp{<!-- cairn:end -->} markers. Running it
again rewrites the block in place and leaves the rest of the file alone, so it
can be re-run whenever the schema changes. Because it is generated, it cannot
describe a workflow the project does not have.

An agent then reads with @samp{cairn list --json} and
@samp{cairn show @var{id} --json}, writes with @command{cairn new} and
@command{cairn set}, and is checked by @command{cairn check} like everyone
else.

@node Continuous integration
@chapter Continuous integration

@example
cairn check --render --strict
@end example

@noindent
This validates every item against the schema and proves that the committed
roadmap matches them. It exits non-zero on any error, and with
@option{--strict} on any warning. Nothing about it requires a particular
forge; it is one command in whatever runs your tests.

@node Identifiers
@chapter Identifiers

@section How an identifier is written

@cindex project key
@cindex id_format
@vindex id_format
By default an identifier reads @code{0001}. A project can say otherwise:

@example
[project]
id_format = "@{n:04@}"           # 0001         --- the default
id_format = "MP-@{n@}"           # MP-1002
id_format = "A@{n@}"             # A24
id_format = "CAIRN-TOOLS-@{n@}"  # CAIRN-TOOLS-24
@end example

@noindent
@code{@{n@}} is the number and @code{@{n:04@}} pads it, which is the syntax the
audience already knows from every format string they use. One template rather
than three settings for key, separator and padding: three would express the same
thing less clearly and would allow combinations nobody wants, while a template
shows you what it produces.

@subsection The key is a rendering, not the identifier

@cindex rendering
This is the whole design, and it is worth being explicit about because the
obvious reading of ``I want @code{MP-1002}'' is the wrong answer.

Putting @code{MP-1002} in the frontmatter would make @code{id} not an unsigned
integer, which the specification commits to --- so it would be a new format
number and a migration for every existing project. It would also break the
machinery that depends on identifiers being numbers: allocation is
@code{max + 1}, @command{renumber} compares and reassigns, @code{depends_on} is
a list of integers, and import maps identifiers across projects.

So @code{id} stays an integer and the project declares how it is @emph{written}.
That is not a compromise forced by the format; it is the better design, and the
format promise is what made it obvious. A project changing its key changes how
things are displayed, not what anything is, and nothing that refers to an item
by number breaks.

In JSON, @code{id} is the number and @code{ref} carries the rendered form.

@subsection Typing one

Both forms are accepted, in every place an identifier is taken:

@example
cairn show MP-1002
cairn show 1002
cairn show mp-1002
cairn set MP-1002 depends_on+=MP-1000
@end example

@noindent
Requiring a prefix somebody already knows is friction for nothing, and every
reference written before a project adopted a key is a bare number. Prefix
matching is case-insensitive.

A prefix cannot begin with a digit, and cairn refuses one that does: it would
make the rendered form indistinguishable from a plain number, and two spellings
must not be able to mean different items.

@subsection Where to start counting

@vindex id_start
Allocation is one more than the highest identifier in use, so an empty project's
first item is @code{1}. A team wanting @code{MP-1002} would otherwise have to
create a thousand items.

@example
[project]
id_start = 1000
@end example

@noindent
This applies to an empty project and nowhere else. Lowering it later does
nothing, because allocation still takes the maximum --- which is the right
behaviour, and is said here rather than left to be discovered.

@subsection Adopting a format in an existing project

Filenames carry the rendered identifier, so changing the format means every file
should be renamed. @command{cairn check} reports each mismatch, and
@command{cairn renumber} fixes them; @option{--dry-run} shows what it would do.

@example
cairn check                 # filename does not match the item ...
cairn renumber --dry-run
cairn renumber
@end example

@noindent
Renaming is the same job as repairing a duplicate identifier --- making the
identifiers on disk say what they mean --- which is why it lives in the same
command rather than a new one.

@subsection Reading an identifier out of a filename

@cindex filename fallback
The specification allows a reader to take @code{id} from a leading run of digits
when the key is absent. @file{MP-1002-slug.md} has no leading digits, so that
fallback alone would make a hand-written file in such a project unreadable ---
and silently, which is the wrong failure. cairn also tries the project's own
rendering, which §4.2 of the specification permits.


@cindex ids
@cindex merge conflicts
Item ids are small integers because people have to type them. That choice has
a cost, and it is worth stating plainly.

A sequential id is allocated as one more than the highest in use, which
requires knowing every id in use --- that is, it requires coordination. A
distributed workflow does not provide any. Two contributors on two branches
each create @dfn{the next} item and both get the same number. The branches
merge cleanly, because the filenames differ, and the repository now holds two
items sharing an id.

@command{cairn check} detects this and names both files.
@command{cairn renumber} repairs it: the first file to hold the id, in a
deterministic order, keeps it, and the others are reassigned. Because nothing
can unambiguously refer to a duplicated id, existing @code{depends_on}
references are left pointing at the retained item and a note says so.

@example
$ cairn renumber --dry-run
  0002 -> 0003  Branch B feature
$ cairn renumber
@end example

@cindex compacting ids
@option{--compact} renumbers every item into a gapless sequence, rewriting
@code{depends_on} references as it goes. It refuses to run while duplicates
exist, since which item a reference meant would then be a guess. Compacting
rewrites most of the item directory, so it is worth doing rarely and on a
clean tree.

Renumbering is never automatic. It rewrites files, and that should happen
because you asked.

